WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.017
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4485.

2
00:00:01.017 --> 00:00:02.033
Begin Session III.]

3
00:00:02.033 --> 00:00:02.711
<v CHELSEA ARSENEAULT>Ready Kyle?

4
00:00:02.711 --> 00:00:03.050
Alrighty.

5
00:00:03.050 --> 00:00:05.421
I am here with Ms. Lenoria today.

6
00:00:05.421 --> 00:00:07.116
It is Friday July tenth.

7
00:00:07.116 --> 00:00:09.149
This is our third interview together.

8
00:00:09.149 --> 00:00:11.182
Second one in her lovely home.

9
00:00:11.182 --> 00:00:13.215
We're doing a follow up interview.

10
00:00:13.215 --> 00:00:16.264
Just kind of picking up where we left off.

11
00:00:16.264 --> 00:00:25.413
Last time you were just telling us about the history of Mossville and your family and your role being the first black woman working in the plant.

12
00:00:25.413 --> 00:00:27.785
And what that was like and everything.

13
00:00:27.785 --> 00:00:36.934
I kind of wanted to pick up with that a little bit and ask you a little bit more about what it was like working at Olin.

14
00:00:36.934 --> 00:00:37.950
<v LENORIA AMBROSE>Olin [chemical company].

15
00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:38.289
<v ARSENEAULT>Olin.

16
00:00:38.289 --> 00:00:39.306
Is it O-L-A-N?

17
00:00:39.306 --> 00:00:39.645
<v AMBROSE>O-L-I-N.

18
00:00:39.645 --> 00:00:39.983
<v ARSENEAULT>O-L-I-N.

19
00:00:39.983 --> 00:00:41.000
<v AMBROSE>Yes, Olin.

20
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:41.434
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

21
00:00:41.434 --> 00:00:42.301
<v AMBROSE>Olin Mathieson.

22
00:00:42.301 --> 00:00:44.036
It was Olin Mathieson.

23
00:00:44.036 --> 00:00:46.205
Which was an alkali plant.

24
00:00:46.205 --> 00:00:47.072
Chlor-alkali plant.

25
00:00:47.072 --> 00:00:49.675
And it came here in 1934.

26
00:00:49.675 --> 00:00:52.277
And then it became Olin Corporation.

27
00:00:52.277 --> 00:00:56.181
And then we were bought out by Arco [Chemical].

28
00:00:56.181 --> 00:01:10.928
And then we went from Arco to Lyondell [Petrochemical Company] and part of our plant was bought out by . . . We had a pace plant which was a plant that sold chemicals for swimming pools.

29
00:01:10.928 --> 00:01:14.831
So somebody bought that part of the plant out,

30
00:01:14.831 --> 00:01:22.205
and then another part of the plant that had . . . We had a [ RF?] 230 and T101 plant.

31
00:01:22.205 --> 00:01:30.446
Those plants were used to make the foam that goes in as insulation in refrigerators, and freezers, and cars.

32
00:01:30.446 --> 00:01:32.614
The foam that we have.

33
00:01:32.614 --> 00:01:39.988
And then of course, we made, our main product was urea which was used as a fertilizer.

34
00:01:39.988 --> 00:01:46.060
Ammonia, nitric acid, we had TDI, which is also used in the foaming industry.

35
00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:53.000
Luxate, and of course we had other chemicals that were made to make those products.

36
00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:58.619
So we had products brought into us and we made products to make the product.

37
00:01:58.619 --> 00:02:06.860
So that was the beginning, and Olin was a . . . When I first started working there, there were about I guess eight-hundred people.

38
00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:13.603
And as time went on, and as far as the office workers when I first started working there,

39
00:02:13.603 --> 00:02:17.349
were about sixty some people that was in the union.

40
00:02:17.349 --> 00:02:18.473
We were unionized.

41
00:02:18.473 --> 00:02:22.968
And then there was another thirty or forty people that were nonunion.

42
00:02:22.968 --> 00:02:24.841
Like the plant manager's secretary.

43
00:02:24.841 --> 00:02:26.340
Our chem lab secretary.

44
00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:28.213
Those people, they were nonunion.

45
00:02:28.213 --> 00:02:32.708
But as time went on, in 1992, they started laying off people.

46
00:02:32.708 --> 00:02:37.578
If somebody retired, they kind of took over more parts of the plant.

47
00:02:37.578 --> 00:02:43.571
One time, when I first started working out at Olin, I started off in the warehouses.

48
00:02:43.571 --> 00:02:45.444
I'd work in the warehouse.

49
00:02:45.444 --> 00:02:48.816
And we didn't have Xerox machines during that time.

50
00:02:48.816 --> 00:02:50.314
We had manual typewriters.

51
00:02:50.314 --> 00:02:51.813
It was no computers.

52
00:02:51.813 --> 00:02:52.937
That was 1966.

53
00:02:52.937 --> 00:02:57.806
We had the . . . I don't know if you . . . You were probably too young.

54
00:02:57.806 --> 00:03:03.425
Maybe you heard your mom or somebody talk about it, but, we had carbon copies.

55
00:03:03.425 --> 00:03:07.546
You'd put all these carbon copies between the sheets and type.

56
00:03:07.546 --> 00:03:08.670
We had that.

57
00:03:08.670 --> 00:03:09.044
And

58
00:03:09.044 --> 00:03:11.292
<c.p>in 1982, we went to computers.

59
00:03:11.292 --> 00:03:13.540
Of course we had mimeograph machines.

60
00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:19.159
I don't know if you've heard anybody talk about that, before we got Xerox machines.

61
00:03:19.159 --> 00:03:29.648
And they had to use this chemical that they would mix and put it on the little thing as . . . Put the paper in there and run it through.

62
00:03:29.648 --> 00:03:30.771
Very old timey.

63
00:03:30.771 --> 00:03:33.394
So that was before all of that.

64
00:03:33.394 --> 00:03:37.514
I was able to get in the mix of all that.

65
00:03:37.514 --> 00:03:40.511
But in 1982, we went to the computers.

66
00:03:40.511 --> 00:03:46.130
And like I said, in 1992 they started trimming down and laying off and attrition.

67
00:03:46.130 --> 00:03:51.000
When somebody would retire or leave a plant, they wouldn't hire anybody.

68
00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:56.829
So I started, like I said, in the warehouse, and I went to maintenance,

69
00:03:56.829 --> 00:04:02.658
and then in 1973 I went to the TDI plant as a production clerk.

70
00:04:02.658 --> 00:04:07.237
And at that time, I worked only for RF230 and T101.

71
00:04:07.237 --> 00:04:17.230
Well later on, I had to move to the TDI plant and I had RF230, T101, and Luxate, and some of the other plants.

72
00:04:17.230 --> 00:04:26.389
Well, by the time I left Olin or Olin closed, I was doing nitric acid production, TDI production, TDA production phosgene production.

73
00:04:26.389 --> 00:04:33.467
They got rid of all the clerks, and I was the only one left doing production work.

74
00:04:33.467 --> 00:04:39.296
So I had like five or six plants, and I did all their production,

75
00:04:39.296 --> 00:04:45.541
all of their inventory control, the shipping, anything that went in and out the plant.

76
00:04:45.541 --> 00:04:49.288
And we produced, I guess two-hundred-thousand pounds a year.

77
00:04:49.288 --> 00:04:53.868
Two-hundred-thousand . . . I'm sorry two-hundred something million pounds a year of TDI.

78
00:04:53.868 --> 00:04:56.782
So it was a lot of work.

79
00:04:56.782 --> 00:04:59.696
Plus I worked for all the guys.

80
00:04:59.696 --> 00:05:03.027
Order their materials for them that they needed.

81
00:05:03.027 --> 00:05:04.693
All the raw materials.

82
00:05:04.693 --> 00:05:06.774
I did all of that.

83
00:05:06.774 --> 00:05:10.938
So I was working sixteen . . . Twelve to sixteen hour days.

84
00:05:10.938 --> 00:05:12.603
Seven days a week.

85
00:05:12.603 --> 00:05:15.518
And it was just . . . It was brutal.

86
00:05:15.518 --> 00:05:19.681
But that was the way they cut back on costs,

87
00:05:19.681 --> 00:05:27.175
and then in 2006 when the plant shut down, it was costing us more for our raw materials.

88
00:05:27.175 --> 00:05:32.588
They had a cap set on what they could sell the materials for.

89
00:05:32.588 --> 00:05:35.918
So they ended up shutting the plant down.

90
00:05:35.918 --> 00:05:38.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Where was it located?

91
00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:41.181
<v AMBROSE>On I-10 when you go over the I-10 bridge.

92
00:05:41.181 --> 00:05:43.302
Right on the left hand side.

93
00:05:43.302 --> 00:05:45.423
That was, all that was Olin.

94
00:05:45.423 --> 00:05:48.605
You'll probably see the production building is still there.

95
00:05:48.605 --> 00:05:51.079
And you might see something called Chemtura.

96
00:05:51.079 --> 00:05:53.200
That was all part of us.

97
00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:56.028
It was called Bio Lab at that time.

98
00:05:56.028 --> 00:06:02.037
And then they had . . . We also produced something called hydrazine, which was used in the space industry.

99
00:06:02.037 --> 00:06:05.926
It used to fuel the rocket . . . It was a rocket fuel.

100
00:06:05.926 --> 00:06:09.107
So that was part of our production stream too.

101
00:06:09.107 --> 00:06:11.581
I didn't ever have to do that.

102
00:06:11.581 --> 00:06:14.056
There was always somebody that did that.

103
00:06:14.056 --> 00:06:17.591
But we had a lot of chemicals that we made.

104
00:06:17.591 --> 00:06:21.126
And of course we had the bad, bad chemical, phosgene.

105
00:06:21.126 --> 00:06:24.307
I don’t know if you've ever heard of that.

106
00:06:24.307 --> 00:06:26.781
We used that in our TDI production.

107
00:06:26.781 --> 00:06:31.730
So we had a whole bunch of smaller plants that fueled the TDI plant.

108
00:06:31.730 --> 00:06:33.851
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it one of the first?

109
00:06:33.851 --> 00:06:34.912
Like how long . . .?

110
00:06:34.912 --> 00:06:38.093
<v AMBROSE>It was the first chemical plant in the area.

111
00:06:38.093 --> 00:06:44.456
But then of course we got Conoco and PPG and all the other plants came in after this.

112
00:06:44.456 --> 00:06:45.163
Cities Service.

113
00:06:45.163 --> 00:06:48.698
But Olin was the first chemical plant in the area.

114
00:06:48.698 --> 00:06:50.465
Like I said, in 1934.

115
00:06:50.465 --> 00:06:51.879
<v ARSENEAULT>How did that happen?

116
00:06:51.879 --> 00:06:54.000
How did it come to . . .?

117
00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:56.363
<v AMBROSE>You know I don't really know.

118
00:06:56.363 --> 00:06:59.907
I think they, we were in a good location.

119
00:06:59.907 --> 00:07:03.845
We had the water, where they could bring barges in.

120
00:07:03.845 --> 00:07:05.420
We had the railroad.

121
00:07:05.420 --> 00:07:06.995
We had the interstate.

122
00:07:06.995 --> 00:07:12.114
So at that time it wasn't the interstate, but they had good highways.

123
00:07:12.114 --> 00:07:17.233
And I think the thing that really sold them was probably the water.

124
00:07:17.233 --> 00:07:26.684
Because they could bring ships and barges in to bring their produce in that they needed to . . . the raw materials in that they needed.

125
00:07:26.684 --> 00:07:28.259
And I guess you

126
00:07:28.259 --> 00:07:29.834
<c.p>know how they do.

127
00:07:29.834 --> 00:07:34.560
They have these people that go out hunting for these different plants.

128
00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:39.679
And they just happen to . . . Because I don't remember anybody ever really saying,

129
00:07:39.679 --> 00:07:45.192
when we had some of the beginning people come here and talk about Olin.

130
00:07:45.192 --> 00:07:51.886
But I knew that we were an ideal place, because it was the south, the warm weather.

131
00:07:51.886 --> 00:07:54.642
They didn't have to worry about freezing.

132
00:07:54.642 --> 00:07:56.611
Chemicals freezing and plants freezing.

133
00:07:56.611 --> 00:07:57.399
Pipes freezing.

134
00:07:57.399 --> 00:07:59.368
And we . . . Our natural resources.

135
00:07:59.368 --> 00:08:00.943
We had natural gas.

136
00:08:00.943 --> 00:08:02.124
Electricity was good.

137
00:08:02.124 --> 00:08:10.000
So we had a lot of things that they needed, that made us look good for the chemical industry.

138
00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:13.045
And it thrived for many, many, many years.

139
00:08:13.045 --> 00:08:15.710
But then in 2006 we shut down.

140
00:08:15.710 --> 00:08:18.375
And like I said, we were union.

141
00:08:18.375 --> 00:08:21.040
The guys in the field were union.

142
00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:25.227
And when I first came into the plant, I was union.

143
00:08:25.227 --> 00:08:29.415
By the time it was shut down I'd become company . . . salaried.

144
00:08:29.415 --> 00:08:35.506
But I was . . . I belonged to the OPEIU which is a part of the AFL CIO.

145
00:08:35.506 --> 00:08:37.409
Was office and professionals union.

146
00:08:37.409 --> 00:08:43.881
And I was the first black to have a . . . become secretary or to become on the board.

147
00:08:43.881 --> 00:08:53.017
And I was able to go to a lot of the conventions that they had, and then, Louisiana became a right to work state.

148
00:08:53.017 --> 00:08:54.920
And you couldn't get contracts.

149
00:08:54.920 --> 00:08:57.966
It was hard to get a contract negotiated.

150
00:08:57.966 --> 00:08:59.489
It was just awful.

151
00:08:59.489 --> 00:09:06.341
Well a lot of people, and I never could understand why they didn't, more people didn't see that.

152
00:09:06.341 --> 00:09:10.148
But the union people drove what the salary people made.

153
00:09:10.148 --> 00:09:13.574
Because they always had to make more than us.

154
00:09:13.574 --> 00:09:19.665
So whatever we got, they had to raise the salary people in order to keep up.

155
00:09:19.665 --> 00:09:28.040
And then the salary people were always so against the union, but they didn't realize that we were kind of an asset,

156
00:09:28.040 --> 00:09:35.653
because as we got raises, they got raises too because they wouldn't let us make more money than the salary.

157
00:09:35.653 --> 00:09:36.415
You know?

158
00:09:36.415 --> 00:09:42.506
It was kind of a . . . Kind of bad in some ways, but good in other ways.

159
00:09:42.506 --> 00:09:47.835
And it was just good all the way around, because we needed the union,

160
00:09:47.835 --> 00:09:54.688
if we didn't have the union we'd probably be making two dollars and fifty cents an hour still.

161
00:09:54.688 --> 00:09:56.972
Because they wouldn't have gone up.

162
00:09:56.972 --> 00:09:58.494
They didn't have to.

163
00:09:58.494 --> 00:10:05.347
<c.p>But when I first started working at Olin I was making thirty . . . three dollars and something an hour.

164
00:10:05.347 --> 00:10:06.869
It was in 1966.

165
00:10:06.869 --> 00:10:09.153
And I thought I was rich.

166
00:10:09.153 --> 00:10:15.244
Bought our first car, was like nineteen-hundred dollars and our second car was thirty-six hundred dollars.

167
00:10:15.244 --> 00:10:18.290
Think about what we paying for cars now.

168
00:10:18.290 --> 00:10:22.858
But in the area, we were making more than the school teachers.

169
00:10:22.858 --> 00:10:25.903
So we had a . . . It drove our economy.

170
00:10:25.903 --> 00:10:30.091
But, and then in 1992, they put a cap on salaries.

171
00:10:30.091 --> 00:10:36.562
If you were coming in as a union employee you were kind of . . . There was a ceiling.

172
00:10:36.562 --> 00:10:40.750
You hit that ceiling and then you couldn’t move any further.

173
00:10:40.750 --> 00:10:43.034
You couldn't make any more money.

174
00:10:43.034 --> 00:10:50.648
So that was a bad thing, when they got the right to work and started doing away with the unions.

175
00:10:50.648 --> 00:10:53.693
But while it lasted, it was really good.

176
00:10:53.693 --> 00:10:59.023
People were able to make a good, good salary and feed our local economy.

177
00:10:59.023 --> 00:11:00.545
We were buying things.

178
00:11:00.545 --> 00:11:01.307
Buying homes.

179
00:11:01.307 --> 00:11:02.068
Buying cars.

180
00:11:02.068 --> 00:11:06.256
And nowadays I look at that and I think about that.

181
00:11:06.256 --> 00:11:12.347
I say, "If they didn't have that I don't know what our area would be like.

182
00:11:12.347 --> 00:11:15.011
Because it would kind of be dead."

183
00:11:15.011 --> 00:11:18.818
If you didn't have the people working at the plants.

184
00:11:18.818 --> 00:11:22.625
That was kind of driving the economy of the community.

185
00:11:22.625 --> 00:11:25.290
So we have so many plants now.

186
00:11:25.290 --> 00:11:27.574
We just have gobs of plants.

187
00:11:27.574 --> 00:11:28.716
Which is good.

188
00:11:28.716 --> 00:11:31.000
Which is a good thing.

189
00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:33.911
<v ARSENEAULT>Before Olin came, where did people work?

190
00:11:33.911 --> 00:11:38.070
<v AMBROSE>Before Olin came . . . A long time ago they had sawmills.

191
00:11:38.070 --> 00:11:40.981
In Sulphur, there was a sulphur mine.

192
00:11:40.981 --> 00:11:42.229
They had farms.

193
00:11:42.229 --> 00:11:43.893
They did farm work.

194
00:11:43.893 --> 00:11:49.715
They . . . Well my grandfather . . . My family . . . My grandfather and his brothers were all carpenters.

195
00:11:49.715 --> 00:11:54.706
And so, of course they never had a problem finding a job.

196
00:11:54.706 --> 00:12:00.944
And then when my uncles came up they . . . some of them went into the plants.

197
00:12:00.944 --> 00:12:03.023
The plants were here then.

198
00:12:03.023 --> 00:12:12.589
But there was still a lot of our people had to leave Louisiana and go other places in order to make a living,

199
00:12:12.589 --> 00:12:17.164
and in order to make a living that they could live

200
00:12:17.164 --> 00:12:19.243
<c.p>on, and a reasonable living.

201
00:12:19.243 --> 00:12:22.986
Because of the wages here were always so low.

202
00:12:22.986 --> 00:12:27.561
That's what happened to my mother in 1990 . . . 1964 . . . in 1962.

203
00:12:27.561 --> 00:12:34.215
I think my first uncle went out to California, and opened a carpet company out there.

204
00:12:34.215 --> 00:12:37.958
And then, he started sending for my other uncles.

205
00:12:37.958 --> 00:12:39.621
My mother's other brothers.

206
00:12:39.621 --> 00:12:45.444
And they went out there, because they could not make a good enough living.

207
00:12:45.444 --> 00:12:54.178
They were working in the plants, but they were working not for the plant but as contract work for the plant.

208
00:12:54.178 --> 00:12:56.673
So their salaries weren't that high.

209
00:12:56.673 --> 00:13:00.000
So they left and went to California.

210
00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:07.133
Then in 1970, my mom went out for vacation with my three younger siblings, and didn't come back.

211
00:13:07.133 --> 00:13:11.096
When she got there they had a house for her.

212
00:13:11.096 --> 00:13:13.473
They had a job for her.

213
00:13:13.473 --> 00:13:18.625
Because she would never have left otherwise, because my grandfather was still here.

214
00:13:18.625 --> 00:13:26.154
And of course, he was getting older and she didn't want to leave home and you know leave him.

215
00:13:26.154 --> 00:13:33.684
But they had so many things available for her, and already set up she just felt guilty not staying.

216
00:13:33.684 --> 00:13:40.024
And then they came here and picked her furniture up and moved her out to California.

217
00:13:40.024 --> 00:13:45.572
So she left and went on vacation, and that was the end of that.

218
00:13:45.572 --> 00:13:46.364
She stayed.

219
00:13:46.364 --> 00:13:55.479
And my mother, she was working at Holly Hills Nursing Home as a nurse's aide, and the wages were very, very, very low.

220
00:13:55.479 --> 00:14:01.027
But she went out there, and she like tripled what she was making here.

221
00:14:01.027 --> 00:14:07.763
So in a way my younger siblings came up out there, and life was a lot different.

222
00:14:07.763 --> 00:14:14.896
I mean of course you had rent to pay there, and you didn't have rent to pay here.

223
00:14:14.896 --> 00:14:22.822
Food costs there was low, because they have so many farms in California, and produce and everything was higher . . . low.

224
00:14:22.822 --> 00:14:26.785
But then the taxes, and property taxes, were astronomically high.

225
00:14:26.785 --> 00:14:30.351
So some things were higher some things were lower.

226
00:14:30.351 --> 00:14:37.484
But the kids got a good education and there was just so much more to see out there.

227
00:14:37.484 --> 00:14:39.069
And do out there.

228
00:14:39.069 --> 00:14:40.258
So she stayed.

229
00:14:40.258 --> 00:14:47.787
But she grew up in Mossville, and my whole family grew up in Mossville but as they got older,

230
00:14:47.787 --> 00:14:50.957
when they were older, then they migrated out

231
00:14:50.957 --> 00:14:51.750
<c.p>to California.

232
00:14:51.750 --> 00:14:58.487
And that's the . . . My great-grandfather, his children [Ellison?] Perkins, which was Minerva's son, he went out there.

233
00:14:58.487 --> 00:15:05.620
His children went out there when they were young, because there was nothing for them to do here.

234
00:15:05.620 --> 00:15:07.997
So they all migrated to California.

235
00:15:07.997 --> 00:15:11.564
So half of our family was in California already.

236
00:15:11.564 --> 00:15:17.508
Because they could make a good living and they didn't have to struggle as hard.

237
00:15:17.508 --> 00:15:18.301
Minerva's children.

238
00:15:18.301 --> 00:15:22.263
Well his older girls were the first ones that went.

239
00:15:22.263 --> 00:15:22.660
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

240
00:15:22.660 --> 00:15:27.811
<v AMBROSE>They're older . . . Well, his . . . He had two children before he married [Colonia?] Braxton.

241
00:15:27.811 --> 00:15:29.000
<v ARSENAULT>Oh Okay [ . . .?]

242
00:15:29.000 --> 00:15:30.333
<v AMBROSE>He had two children.

243
00:15:30.333 --> 00:15:34.333
So those girls went out to California when they got of age.

244
00:15:34.333 --> 00:15:34.667
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

245
00:15:34.667 --> 00:15:36.667
<v AMBROSE>Then of course the other kids . . .

246
00:15:36.667 --> 00:15:38.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What year was that?

247
00:15:38.000 --> 00:15:38.667
<v AMBROSE>Oh God.

248
00:15:38.667 --> 00:15:39.667
I don't know.

249
00:15:39.667 --> 00:15:41.667
It was a long time ago.

250
00:15:41.667 --> 00:15:43.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Before your mom?

251
00:15:43.000 --> 00:15:44.871
<v AMBROSE>Before . . . I'm trying to think.

252
00:15:44.871 --> 00:15:50.859
My mother was born in 1928, and it might have been after my mom was born.

253
00:15:50.859 --> 00:15:56.098
But it was . . . I guess she might have been a teenager when they moved.

254
00:15:56.098 --> 00:15:56.847
Or younger.

255
00:15:56.847 --> 00:15:58.718
<v ARSENEAULT>Why'd they move out there?

256
00:15:58.718 --> 00:16:00.963
<v AMBROSE>Because times were really hard here.

257
00:16:00.963 --> 00:16:03.209
<v ARSENEAULT>In like the 1930s and so?

258
00:16:03.209 --> 00:16:05.454
<v AMBROSE>It was hard to find jobs.

259
00:16:05.454 --> 00:16:07.325
1920s . . . I guess the depression.

260
00:16:07.325 --> 00:16:07.699
Twenties.

261
00:16:07.699 --> 00:16:08.074
Twenty-eight.

262
00:16:08.074 --> 00:16:13.687
My mother was born in (nineteen) twenty-eight and then twenty-eight my mother said the depression.

263
00:16:13.687 --> 00:16:17.429
They were still living in the throes of the depression.

264
00:16:17.429 --> 00:16:21.546
So a lot of them left here and went out there.

265
00:16:21.546 --> 00:16:24.540
Because life was a lot better out there.

266
00:16:24.540 --> 00:16:28.282
<v ARSENEAULT>So a lot of people from Mossville went to California?

267
00:16:28.282 --> 00:16:29.779
<v AMBROSE>A lot of people.

268
00:16:29.779 --> 00:16:31.276
A lot of people.

269
00:16:31.276 --> 00:16:35.018
And a lot of families lost family members to California.

270
00:16:35.018 --> 00:16:41.755
And of course other parts of the country, but I think more went to California than anywhere else.

271
00:16:41.755 --> 00:16:44.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did they ever come back?

272
00:16:44.000 --> 00:16:45.799
<v AMBROSE>No. They died out there.

273
00:16:45.799 --> 00:16:54.796
Or their children were all born out there so they had no reason to come . . . Some of the young ones . . . My generation went out there,

274
00:16:54.796 --> 00:16:56.595
and they've come back home.

275
00:16:56.595 --> 00:17:03.073
A lot of the people . . . I don't know if you've talked to any, Walter Perkins and Gloria Perkins?

276
00:17:03.073 --> 00:17:03.433
<v ARSENEAULT>No.

277
00:17:03.433 --> 00:17:05.952
<v AMBROSE>They live right down past the church.

278
00:17:05.952 --> 00:17:14.588
But they were the first ones . . . I think they might have been the first ones that went out to California when they were young,

279
00:17:14.588 --> 00:17:16.747
and then they came back home.

280
00:17:16.747 --> 00:17:20.346
So we have several that went out and came home.

281
00:17:20.346 --> 00:17:21.426
After they retired.

282
00:17:21.426 --> 00:17:25.384
But they were able to own their own companies out there.

283
00:17:25.384 --> 00:17:30.422
You know, they, a lot of them worked for the telephone company out there.

284
00:17:30.422 --> 00:17:36.540
The ones from San Francisco worked for the . . . I guess it was the . . . Was it the city?

285
00:17:36.540 --> 00:17:38.699
It might have been the state.

286
00:17:38.699 --> 00:17:40.498
On the Golden Gate bridge.

287
00:17:40.498 --> 00:17:41.938
I have a half-brother.

288
00:17:41.938 --> 00:17:46.616
My father's . . . My father, when he left from here he went out there,

289
00:17:46.616 --> 00:17:50.574
and he worked as a bus driver for the rapid transit.

290
00:17:50.574 --> 00:17:56.332
And then he has a son who worked as a painter on the Golden Gate Bridge.

291
00:17:56.332 --> 00:17:57.772
He was a supervisor.

292
00:17:57.772 --> 00:18:00.291
So they had different jobs out there.

293
00:18:00.291 --> 00:18:02.450
But they just made more money.

294
00:18:02.450 --> 00:18:06.408
And like I was . . . I don't know if I told you,

295
00:18:06.408 --> 00:18:12.166
my dad, when he was here, he was the first black to run for police juror.

296
00:18:12.166 --> 00:18:17.924
His name was Gradis Braxton, and he was the first black to run for police juror.

297
00:18:17.924 --> 00:18:21.163
He was running for ward four across the lake.

298
00:18:21.163 --> 00:18:26.920
My father had a cleaners, he had a grocery store, and he had a furniture store.

299
00:18:26.920 --> 00:18:28.000
<v ARSENEAULT>In Mossville?

300
00:18:28.000 --> 00:18:28.824
<v AMBROSE>In Mossville.

301
00:18:28.824 --> 00:18:30.882
Right down past the school.

302
00:18:30.882 --> 00:18:32.941
You're familiar with Bel Air?

303
00:18:32.941 --> 00:18:33.765
Michigan Avenue?

304
00:18:33.765 --> 00:18:41.588
Well between where the Sasol property line is there, and Michigan Avenue, well his store was in that area.

305
00:18:41.588 --> 00:18:42.412
Right there.

306
00:18:42.412 --> 00:18:46.941
He had the furniture store, the grocery store, and the cleaners.

307
00:18:46.941 --> 00:18:56.412
But because of the economy . . . The economy was so poor that people in Mossville were, you know, they just made a living barely.

308
00:18:56.412 --> 00:18:59.294
Then he left and went to California.

309
00:18:59.294 --> 00:19:00.529
<v ARSENEAULT>When was that?

310
00:19:00.529 --> 00:19:02.588
<v AMBROSE>He left in . . . Let’s see.

311
00:19:02.588 --> 00:19:04.235
I graduated in sixty-four.

312
00:19:04.235 --> 00:19:08.353
He must have gone out there in the early sixties.

313
00:19:08.353 --> 00:19:10.000
In the early sixties.

314
00:19:10.000 --> 00:19:12.471
<v ARSENEAULT>How long did his store operate?

315
00:19:12.471 --> 00:19:19.471
<v AMBROSE>Oh gosh I was young and I don't really remember how many years he had that store.

316
00:19:19.471 --> 00:19:28.118
I'd have to find out from his sister how many years he had that store, because I don't . . . I was young.

317
00:19:28.118 --> 00:19:31.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you ever go and visit?

318
00:19:31.000 --> 00:19:32.941
<v AMBROSE>I would go down there sometimes.

319
00:19:32.941 --> 00:19:33.265
Yes.

320
00:19:33.265 --> 00:19:35.853
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you ever help him out or anything?

321
00:19:35.853 --> 00:19:36.176
<v AMBROSE>No.

322
00:19:36.176 --> 00:19:36.500
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh.

323
00:19:36.500 --> 00:19:37.794
<v AMBROSE>I was too young.

324
00:19:37.794 --> 00:19:38.118
No.

325
00:19:38.118 --> 00:19:39.088
<v ARSENEAULT>You were little?

326
00:19:39.088 --> 00:19:42.000
<v AMBROSE>And my grandfather wouldn't have liked that anyway.

327
00:19:42.000 --> 00:19:42.549
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh really?

328
00:19:42.549 --> 00:19:44.471
<v AMBROSE>No. He didn't believe in us working.

329
00:19:44.471 --> 00:19:45.569
You went to school.

330
00:19:45.569 --> 00:19:48.314
You did what you were supposed to do in school.

331
00:19:48.314 --> 00:19:50.510
You went to church, and that was it.

332
00:19:50.510 --> 00:19:51.608
That was our responsibility.

333
00:19:51.608 --> 00:19:53.255
<v ARSENEAULT>As children or as a woman?

334
00:19:53.255 --> 00:19:53.529
<v AMBROSE>Pardon?

335
00:19:53.529 --> 00:19:54.902
<v ARSENEAULT>As a child or as . . .

336
00:19:54.902 --> 00:19:56.000
<v AMBROSE>As a child.

337
00:19:56.000 --> 00:19:56.794
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay, okay.

338
00:19:56.794 --> 00:19:57.191
<v AMBROSE>Yes.

339
00:19:57.191 --> 00:19:59.574
Oh that just stayed with you.

340
00:19:59.574 --> 00:20:07.915
You got to be an adult you knew what your responsibilities were by the time you got to be an adult.

341
00:20:07.915 --> 00:20:10.695
But he didn't believe in children working.

342
00:20:10.695 --> 00:20:16.652
Because he thought if you worked, you didn't have time to do your school work.

343
00:20:16.652 --> 00:20:22.610
And we had to do well in school, because we had to get an education.

344
00:20:22.610 --> 00:20:23.801
They pushed education.

345
00:20:23.801 --> 00:20:30.156
And I have a . . . I didn't tell you about that the last time you were here.

346
00:20:30.156 --> 00:20:35.319
We have a . . . Like I said, on my grandfather's side were the Towners.

347
00:20:35.319 --> 00:20:38.894
Came from . . . Originally came from the Sugartown, DeRidder area.

348
00:20:38.894 --> 00:20:44.851
They . . . Their mother walked from Meridian, Mississippi . . . This is during the slavery time . . . to DeRidder.

349
00:20:44.851 --> 00:20:46.440
<v ARSENEAULT>How far is that?

350
00:20:46.440 --> 00:20:47.631
<v AMBROSE>Oh my gosh.

351
00:20:47.631 --> 00:20:48.426
Meridian, Mississippi?

352
00:20:48.426 --> 00:20:50.411
That's further than New Orleans.

353
00:20:50.411 --> 00:20:52.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh my goodness.

354
00:20:52.000 --> 00:20:53.788
<v AMBROSE>Because that's in Mississippi.

355
00:20:53.788 --> 00:20:55.128
<v ARSENEAULT>She just walked?

356
00:20:55.128 --> 00:20:56.916
<v AMBROSE>She and her family.

357
00:20:56.916 --> 00:20:57.810
Her children.

358
00:20:57.810 --> 00:21:01.385
Walked from Meridian, Mississippi to the DeRidder area.

359
00:21:01.385 --> 00:21:11.665
And slavery hadn’t ended yet, but they met a . . . and I don't know exactly the whole story, but I didn't think to ask.

360
00:21:11.665 --> 00:21:22.838
But some way they got in touch with the [Kagals?] here in Lake Charles and the Kagals here in Lake Charles had a car dealership.

361
00:21:22.838 --> 00:21:27.754
The Chevrolet car dealership, and he was given my great-great-great grandfather.

362
00:21:27.754 --> 00:21:28.648
Great grandfather.

363
00:21:28.648 --> 00:21:30.436
Double . . . two great great-grandfather.

364
00:21:30.436 --> 00:21:35.799
Was given to one of the Kagal girls when she got married.

365
00:21:35.799 --> 00:21:38.034
That was before slavery ended.

366
00:21:38.034 --> 00:21:41.609
And he was six or seven years old.

367
00:21:41.609 --> 00:21:47.419
And when slavery ended, she sent him back to DeRidder to his dad.

368
00:21:47.419 --> 00:21:49.654
To his mama and dad.

369
00:21:49.654 --> 00:21:52.782
And they stayed in touch with her.

370
00:21:52.782 --> 00:21:55.017
She educated my two great-aunts.

371
00:21:55.017 --> 00:21:55.911
His children.

372
00:21:55.911 --> 00:21:56.804
His daughters.

373
00:21:56.804 --> 00:21:58.592
She believed in education.

374
00:21:58.592 --> 00:22:03.061
Her name was [Forthenia?] Kagal and she believed in education.

375
00:22:03.061 --> 00:22:07.084
And one of my great-great-aunts was named after her.

376
00:22:07.084 --> 00:22:08.872
And she educated them.

377
00:22:08.872 --> 00:22:12.000
She sent them to Southern University.

378
00:22:12.000 --> 00:22:12.364
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

379
00:22:12.364 --> 00:22:13.455
<v AMBROSE>To be educated.

380
00:22:13.455 --> 00:22:15.273
Yes she believed in education.

381
00:22:15.273 --> 00:22:16.364
Especially for girls.

382
00:22:16.364 --> 00:22:16.727
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

383
00:22:16.727 --> 00:22:20.364
<v AMBROSE>Because back then they didn't think girls needed an education.

384
00:22:20.364 --> 00:22:23.273
But my great-great-aunts were educated because of her.

385
00:22:23.273 --> 00:22:28.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So they stayed with her and he went back to his parents?

386
00:22:28.000 --> 00:22:30.489
<v AMBROSE>No, no. He went back to his parents.

387
00:22:30.489 --> 00:22:32.044
He was still a child.

388
00:22:32.044 --> 00:22:32.667
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh okay.

389
00:22:32.667 --> 00:22:34.844
<v AMBROSE>But they stayed in touch with her.

390
00:22:34.844 --> 00:22:36.089
With the Kagal family.

391
00:22:36.089 --> 00:22:36.711
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh okay.

392
00:22:36.711 --> 00:22:39.200
<v AMBROSE>They stayed in touch with the Kagal family.

393
00:22:39.200 --> 00:22:45.111
And when he got . . . When he became an adult and had children, well then she educated his two daughters.

394
00:22:45.111 --> 00:22:46.667
And he had several children.

395
00:22:46.667 --> 00:22:49.467
But she educated . . . She saw that they were educated.

396
00:22:49.467 --> 00:22:56.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What did he end up . . . I was going to ask you what your grandparents did for a living in Mossville?

397
00:22:56.000 --> 00:22:56.650
<v AMBROSE>In Mossville?

398
00:22:56.650 --> 00:22:59.897
My great-grandfather . . . We were talking about that the other day.

399
00:22:59.897 --> 00:23:03.145
One of the things that he did . . . He made coal.

400
00:23:03.145 --> 00:23:03.470
<v ARSENEAULT>Coal?

401
00:23:03.470 --> 00:23:03.795
<v AMBROSE>Charcoal.

402
00:23:03.795 --> 00:23:04.120
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh.

403
00:23:04.120 --> 00:23:04.444
<v AMBROSE>Yes.

404
00:23:04.444 --> 00:23:08.667
They would burn, I guess cut down trees and he had like a….

405
00:23:08.667 --> 00:23:11.915
I had a picture of it and I was wondering.

406
00:23:11.915 --> 00:23:12.889
I asked somebody.

407
00:23:12.889 --> 00:23:14.838
I said, "What is Grandpa doing?"

408
00:23:14.838 --> 00:23:18.735
Because there was this huge pit and it was about so deep.

409
00:23:18.735 --> 00:23:22.308
And they said, "Well he was burning . . . He was making coal."

410
00:23:22.308 --> 00:23:27.504
They would burn trees and then they would make the charcoal out of the burnt trees.

411
00:23:27.504 --> 00:23:30.752
I said, "Well that was. . . That was kind of cool."

412
00:23:30.752 --> 00:23:34.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes I didn't know that's how you did that.

413
00:23:34.000 --> 00:23:40.485
<v AMBROSE>But then he also worked as a . . . When Chennault [Air Force Base] came in, the soldiers at Chennault, they built Maplewood.

414
00:23:40.485 --> 00:23:43.265
And I don't know if you've hear of Maplewood?

415
00:23:43.265 --> 00:23:44.500
<v ARSENEAULT>Is that the camp?

416
00:23:44.500 --> 00:23:46.044
<v AMBROSE>That's right across Highway 90.

417
00:23:46.044 --> 00:23:47.588
It's a subdivision of Sulphur.

418
00:23:47.588 --> 00:23:52.221
Well they were building homes down there for all these airmen that were coming in.

419
00:23:52.221 --> 00:23:54.691
And he worked as a guard down there.

420
00:23:54.691 --> 00:23:56.853
Because I never knew what he did.

421
00:23:56.853 --> 00:24:01.176
As a child you know you never . . . You knew that he went to work.

422
00:24:01.176 --> 00:24:01.485
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

423
00:24:01.485 --> 00:24:06.735
<v AMBROSE>Well by the time we go old enough to realize, he wasn't working anymore he was retired.

424
00:24:06.735 --> 00:24:08.897
Well we never knew what he did.

425
00:24:08.897 --> 00:24:11.985
I just knew all about my uncles and my grandfather

426
00:24:11.985 --> 00:24:12.912
<c.p>were all carpenters.

427
00:24:12.912 --> 00:24:17.544
And they built homes, and they built beautiful cabinets, and they did all this stuff,

428
00:24:17.544 --> 00:24:21.868
but grandpa, he was always at home so we didn't know what he did.

429
00:24:21.868 --> 00:24:23.721
And I was asking my uncle.

430
00:24:23.721 --> 00:24:25.882
I said, "Well what did Grandpa do?"

431
00:24:25.882 --> 00:24:27.735
I said, "Did he ever work?"

432
00:24:27.735 --> 00:24:30.206
Because I said, "I never saw him work."

433
00:24:30.206 --> 00:24:31.441
He said, "Oh yeah."

434
00:24:31.441 --> 00:24:32.676
He said, "He worked."

435
00:24:32.676 --> 00:24:40.088
He said, "He made charcoal and then he made the . . . And then he worked at the saw mill when the saw mill was here.

436
00:24:40.088 --> 00:24:44.721
But the latter part of his life, before it was time for him to retire,

437
00:24:44.721 --> 00:24:48.735
that's when he worked over as a guard over in the Maplewood area."

438
00:24:48.735 --> 00:24:51.515
And then Grandpa Perkins, he worked . . . He did farming.

439
00:24:51.515 --> 00:24:52.750
He was a farmer.

440
00:24:52.750 --> 00:24:56.147
And he worked in the saw mills, when they were here.

441
00:24:56.147 --> 00:25:02.941
And then of course by the time we got old enough, he was already old and he . . . My great-grandfather had a stroke.

442
00:25:02.941 --> 00:25:08.500
And he was at home during the time that we got old enough to, you know, to realize.

443
00:25:08.500 --> 00:25:10.044
So he didn't work anymore.

444
00:25:10.044 --> 00:25:14.676
But, I knew that he did that by my mother telling me that Grandpa worked.

445
00:25:14.676 --> 00:25:19.000
He had a farm and of course he worked in the saw mills.

446
00:25:19.000 --> 00:25:24.848
<v ARSENEAULT>So they were . . . So your father's father was alive when you were growing up?

447
00:25:24.848 --> 00:25:26.101
<v AMBROSE>My mother's grandfather.

448
00:25:26.101 --> 00:25:27.772
My mother's mother's father.

449
00:25:27.772 --> 00:25:28.608
My great-grandfather.

450
00:25:28.608 --> 00:25:29.025
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

451
00:25:29.025 --> 00:25:37.380
<v AMBROSE>But they were all . . . Both of my great-grandparents were . . . My grandfather on my father's side and my mother's mother's side.

452
00:25:37.380 --> 00:25:39.886
<v ARSENEAULT>They were alive when you were . . .?

453
00:25:39.886 --> 00:25:43.228
<v AMBROSE>They were alive when I was growing up.

454
00:25:43.228 --> 00:25:46.987
I was gr own when . . . On my grandfather's side.

455
00:25:46.987 --> 00:25:50.329
When his father died, I was an adult.

456
00:25:50.329 --> 00:25:58.684
And when my . . . When [Ellison?] died I was . . . When he died, I guess I was six or seven years old.

457
00:25:58.684 --> 00:26:00.772
Five or six years old.

458
00:26:00.772 --> 00:26:05.367
But I remember him, because we would visit all the time.

459
00:26:05.367 --> 00:26:09.127
But of course I was grown when she died.

460
00:26:09.127 --> 00:26:12.886
She died in . . . She was ninety-eight when she passed.

461
00:26:12.886 --> 00:26:15.392
But Grandpa had had a stroke.

462
00:26:15.392 --> 00:26:21.241
And I'm not sure exactly how old . . . How old he was when he died.

463
00:26:21.241 --> 00:26:24.165
Let’s see . . . He was born in 1875.

464
00:26:24.165 --> 00:26:25.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

465
00:26:25.000 --> 00:26:28.256
<v AMBROSE>And he died in 1952.

466
00:26:28.256 --> 00:26:30.860
<v ARSENEAULT>So fifteen plus fifty . . .

467
00:26:30.860 --> 00:26:31.512
<v AMBROSE>Seven . . .

468
00:26:31.512 --> 00:26:34.116
<v ARSENEAULT>So he was seventy-three?

469
00:26:34.116 --> 00:26:36.070
Is that right?

470
00:26:36.070 --> 00:26:37.372
Minus two . . .

471
00:26:37.372 --> 00:26:41.930
<v AMBROSE>Let's see five from twelve is seven.

472
00:26:41.930 --> 00:26:47.140
<v ARSENEAULT>Here I can add it on my phone.

473
00:26:47.140 --> 00:26:50.395
<v AMBROSE>Seven from . . . He was seventy . . .

474
00:26:50.395 --> 00:26:51.047
<v ARSENEAULT>Three?

475
00:26:51.047 --> 00:26:51.698
<v AMBROSE>Seventy-seven.

476
00:26:51.698 --> 00:26:53.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Seventy-seven?

477
00:26:53.000 --> 00:26:53.697
<v AMBROSE>Right?

478
00:26:53.697 --> 00:26:55.788
<v ARSENEAULT>Wait so it's . . .

479
00:26:55.788 --> 00:26:57.879
<v AMBROSE>1952 and 1875.

480
00:26:57.879 --> 00:26:59.273
<v ARSENEAULT>So 1952?

481
00:26:59.273 --> 00:27:00.667
<v AMBROSE>From 1875.

482
00:27:00.667 --> 00:27:01.364
<v ARSENEAULT>1875.

483
00:27:01.364 --> 00:27:02.758
Yes seventy-seven.

484
00:27:02.758 --> 00:27:13.212
<v AMBROSE>Yes he was seventy-seven and grandma was born in 1881 and she died in 1975.

485
00:27:13.212 --> 00:27:16.000
<v ARSENEAULT>1975 minus 1881?

486
00:27:16.000 --> 00:27:17.586
<v AMBROSE>So she was ninety-four?

487
00:27:17.586 --> 00:27:17.982
<v ARSENEAULT>Ninety . . .

488
00:27:17.982 --> 00:27:18.378
<v AMBROSE>Ninety-four.

489
00:27:18.378 --> 00:27:18.775
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

490
00:27:18.775 --> 00:27:19.171
<v AMBROSE>Ninety-four.

491
00:27:19.171 --> 00:27:23.928
Okay so it was Mama [Mamie?] was at ninety-eight when she died.

492
00:27:23.928 --> 00:27:24.324
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

493
00:27:24.324 --> 00:27:25.910
<v AMBROSE>My grandfather's second wife.

494
00:27:25.910 --> 00:27:28.288
She was ninety-eight when she died.

495
00:27:28.288 --> 00:27:28.685
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

496
00:27:28.685 --> 00:27:31.856
So what did your grandmother's . . . So your grandparents . . .

497
00:27:31.856 --> 00:27:33.441
<v AMBROSE>They were house keepers.

498
00:27:33.441 --> 00:27:34.631
They didn't work.

499
00:27:34.631 --> 00:27:37.405
They just took care of their homes.

500
00:27:37.405 --> 00:27:41.369
And my grandmother [Bertie Mae?] which is my mother's mother.

501
00:27:41.369 --> 00:27:50.883
The story that I grew up on was that when my grandfather left to go to work every morning, he would kiss her bye,

502
00:27:50.883 --> 00:27:58.018
and he had kind of a guilt trip because the morning . . . The day that she had her stroke,

503
00:27:58.018 --> 00:28:03.964
he was in a hurry, and he forgot to come in and kiss her goodbye.

504
00:28:03.964 --> 00:28:13.874
And the other thing was, he always believed that once you left out of the house, don't ever come back because it was bad luck.

505
00:28:13.874 --> 00:28:16.649
And he had come back for something.

506
00:28:16.649 --> 00:28:19.423
And she had a stroke that day.

507
00:28:19.423 --> 00:28:20.613
And she died.

508
00:28:20.613 --> 00:28:22.198
But they said she

509
00:28:22.198 --> 00:28:26.162
<c.p>would get up in the morning and she would dress.

510
00:28:26.162 --> 00:28:32.505
Put her work clothes on to do her house work, washing and whatever she was doing.

511
00:28:32.505 --> 00:28:36.072
But by the time he was to come home,

512
00:28:36.072 --> 00:28:44.000
she'd bathe and put her starch and ironed dress on and always had her ear rings in her ear.

513
00:28:44.000 --> 00:28:46.934
<v ARSENEAULT>So she always looked nice when he came . . .

514
00:28:46.934 --> 00:28:49.502
<v AMBROSE>Always looked nice when he got home.

515
00:28:49.502 --> 00:28:53.170
And I thought that was so unusual for those times.

516
00:28:53.170 --> 00:28:56.472
But they said that was the way she was.

517
00:28:56.472 --> 00:29:03.074
And my . . . And I remember, I was six years old when she died, but she would iron everything.

518
00:29:03.074 --> 00:29:11.511
The sheets, the pillow cases, and they were . . . the pillow cases were starched and ironed, and the sheets were of course just pressed.

519
00:29:11.511 --> 00:29:16.279
And the like underwear, my grandfather's pajamas, and his boxer shorts, always pressed.

520
00:29:16.279 --> 00:29:17.380
Everything was pressed.

521
00:29:17.380 --> 00:29:21.048
That was the way . . . And they worked hard back then.

522
00:29:21.048 --> 00:29:28.384
I didn't see how they go all that energy to do all that stuff, because they didn't have washing machines,

523
00:29:28.384 --> 00:29:34.253
and they had these big huge pots where they boiled their clothes and kept them white.

524
00:29:34.253 --> 00:29:41.956
But they did all that and I said, "We have all these conveniences and I don't even want to iron it.

525
00:29:41.956 --> 00:29:47.459
I put my stuff in the dryer to get the wrinkles out and I'm off."

526
00:29:47.459 --> 00:29:48.559
But they ironed.

527
00:29:48.559 --> 00:29:49.659
She ironed everything.

528
00:29:49.659 --> 00:29:52.227
And Grandma Perkins was the same way.

529
00:29:52.227 --> 00:29:53.328
She ironed everything.

530
00:29:53.328 --> 00:29:55.895
But they never . . . They didn't have jobs.

531
00:29:55.895 --> 00:29:57.729
They were always at home.

532
00:29:57.729 --> 00:30:02.865
And Grandma Towner, she never . . . I don't remember her ever having a job either.

533
00:30:02.865 --> 00:30:04.699
She was always at home.

534
00:30:04.699 --> 00:30:08.000
I have another book with her in it.

535
00:30:08.000 --> 00:30:11.091
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you spend a lot of time with your grandparents?

536
00:30:11.091 --> 00:30:12.018
<v AMBROSE>Oh Lord yes.

537
00:30:12.018 --> 00:30:12.327
Yes.

538
00:30:12.327 --> 00:30:14.800
I don't eat figs today because of that.

539
00:30:14.800 --> 00:30:16.345
<v ARSENEAULT>You don't do what now?

540
00:30:16.345 --> 00:30:24.073
<v AMBROSE>Eat figs, because my grandfather made us go and pick figs and I didn't think I should have picked them because I didn't eat them.

541
00:30:24.073 --> 00:30:26.545
And I had to go . . . During the summer.

542
00:30:26.545 --> 00:30:28.091
This was during the summer.

543
00:30:28.091 --> 00:30:30.564
And we'd get up early in the morning.

544
00:30:30.564 --> 00:30:33.345
We’d cut . . . Daddy would cut a cantaloupe for us,

545
00:30:33.345 --> 00:30:38.291
and we'd be eating our cantaloupe going through from Old Spanish Trail over to Highway 90.

546
00:30:38.291 --> 00:30:39.527
This is the Towners.

547
00:30:39.527 --> 00:30:40.764
That’s my grandfather’s parents.

548
00:30:40.764 --> 00:30:42.000
<v ARSENEAULT>She was beautiful.

549
00:30:42.000 --> 00:30:44.780
<v AMBROSE>[Irene Viola?] Pots Towner.

550
00:30:44.780 --> 00:30:47.559
And David Duff Towner.

551
00:30:47.559 --> 00:30:51.729
<v ARSENEAULT>What are they dressed up for?

552
00:30:51.729 --> 00:30:53.814
<v AMBROSE>Their fiftieth anniversary.

553
00:30:53.814 --> 00:30:55.203
In 1950.

554
00:30:55.203 --> 00:30:57.288
December of 1950.

555
00:30:57.288 --> 00:30:58.678
<v ARSENEAULT>He's handsome.

556
00:30:58.678 --> 00:31:07.017
<v AMBROSE>So that's my grandfather's parents and these two are my grandmother's parents.

557
00:31:07.017 --> 00:31:13.271
The others I showed you. [Ellison and Loney?] Perkins.

558
00:31:13.271 --> 00:31:17.441
My grandma Perkins was a Braxton.

559
00:31:17.441 --> 00:31:23.000
<v ARSENEAULT>It's hard to keep all this straight.

560
00:31:23.000 --> 00:31:23.919
<v AMBROSE>I know.

561
00:31:23.919 --> 00:31:26.216
She was [Loney?] Braxton Perkins.

562
00:31:26.216 --> 00:31:28.514
And he was Ellison Perkins.

563
00:31:28.514 --> 00:31:38.162
And he was part of the . . . His mother was one of the founders, and her grandfather was one of the founders.

564
00:31:38.162 --> 00:31:43.216
Her grandfather was the first preacher at Mount Zion [Baptist Church].

565
00:31:43.216 --> 00:31:44.135
<v ARSENEAULT>What was . . .?

566
00:31:44.135 --> 00:31:45.054
<v AMBROSE>Griffin Braxton.

567
00:31:45.054 --> 00:31:46.892
<v ARSENEAULT>That was her grandfather?

568
00:31:46.892 --> 00:31:48.730
<v AMBROSE>That was her grandfather.

569
00:31:48.730 --> 00:31:53.784
So he was one of the first families in Mossville, too.

570
00:31:53.784 --> 00:31:57.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did they ever talk about him?

571
00:31:57.000 --> 00:32:01.022
<v AMBROSE>It wasn't part of my . . . He never did say a lot.

572
00:32:01.022 --> 00:32:07.237
I know that he was the first preacher at Mount Zion and I know that Isom Perkins,

573
00:32:07.237 --> 00:32:11.258
his father was one of the first deacons at Mount Zion.

574
00:32:11.258 --> 00:32:16.742
But I don't know a lot about Griffin Braxton, other than I knew his children.

575
00:32:16.742 --> 00:32:19.667
But I didn't know a lot about him.

576
00:32:19.667 --> 00:32:26.247
And I was trying to find somebody in that family to see if they had pictures of him,

577
00:32:26.247 --> 00:32:35.753
but I haven’t been able to find anybody that even had a picture of him so that I would have something to kind of go on.

578
00:32:35.753 --> 00:32:41.968
But I'm still looking, because I'm still working on that side of the family, on the history.

579
00:32:41.968 --> 00:32:45.989
I was working on all of them before my husband died.

580
00:32:45.989 --> 00:32:46.720
But after

581
00:32:46.720 --> 00:32:48.914
<c.p>he passed I just kind of . . .

582
00:32:48.914 --> 00:32:50.376
<v ARSENEAULT>When did he pass?

583
00:32:50.376 --> 00:32:51.473
<v AMBROSE>Two thousand five.

584
00:32:51.473 --> 00:32:56.957
But I had already started on that because we had our family reunion in 2003.

585
00:32:56.957 --> 00:33:00.247
So I had a lot of that already done.

586
00:33:00.247 --> 00:33:01.710
I was trying to . . .

587
00:33:01.710 --> 00:33:05.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Was there a grandparent you were closer to?

588
00:33:05.000 --> 00:33:10.657
<v AMBROSE>No. We spent about as much time with one as we did the others.

589
00:33:10.657 --> 00:33:13.890
I think with these . . . With Grandma . . . These here,

590
00:33:13.890 --> 00:33:21.971
we probably spent more time . . . Well we saw them more often, because they were right across the woods from us.

591
00:33:21.971 --> 00:33:28.437
And grandma . . . That bunch, they were right down the street from us but it was easier,

592
00:33:28.437 --> 00:33:36.114
and we couldn't play on the highway or walk on the highway or anything because it was too dangerous.

593
00:33:36.114 --> 00:33:40.155
So we saw more of them when we were younger.

594
00:33:40.155 --> 00:33:46.620
But then as we got older, we spent about as much time with both of them.

595
00:33:46.620 --> 00:33:49.449
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you have any favorite memories or . . .?

596
00:33:49.449 --> 00:33:54.702
<v AMBROSE>Well one of my . . . This is . . . Oh let me just tell you this.

597
00:33:54.702 --> 00:33:56.318
This is Ellison’s mama.

598
00:33:56.318 --> 00:34:01.167
And it was her husband who was one of the first deacons.

599
00:34:01.167 --> 00:34:03.996
And she was from the Moss family.

600
00:34:03.996 --> 00:34:05.612
<v ARSENEAULT>It's a beautiful picture.

601
00:34:05.612 --> 00:34:06.016
<v AMBROSE>Yes.

602
00:34:06.016 --> 00:34:06.824
That's Granny.

603
00:34:06.824 --> 00:34:09.249
Her name was Minerva Moss Perkins.

604
00:34:09.249 --> 00:34:15.714
So the Mosses and the Perkins and the Braxtons were all the first families of Mossville.

605
00:34:15.714 --> 00:34:25.008
And of course the Rigmaidens and the Ladouxs, but the Perkins and the Mosses were the very first that came and settled in.

606
00:34:25.008 --> 00:34:32.282
She when my great . . . Her husband Isom he died between nineteen . . . He was part of the 1870 census,

607
00:34:32.282 --> 00:34:36.727
but then the 1880 or 1890 census I can't find him.

608
00:34:36.727 --> 00:34:38.343
I think he died.

609
00:34:38.343 --> 00:34:42.384
But she homesteaded a hundred and sixty-seven acres in 1890.

610
00:34:42.384 --> 00:34:43.192
<v ARSENEAULT>She did?

611
00:34:43.192 --> 00:34:44.000
<v AMBROSE>Yes.

612
00:34:44.000 --> 00:34:46.123
<v ARSENEUALT>So women could homestead? [phone rings]

613
00:34:46.123 --> 00:34:47.185
<v AMBROSE>He was dead.

614
00:34:47.185 --> 00:34:48.600
He was dead then.

615
00:34:48.600 --> 00:34:51.785
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you need to get that or should we . . .?

616
00:34:51.785 --> 00:34:52.138
<v AMBROSE>Nah.

617
00:34:52.138 --> 00:34:53.200
I’ll get it.

618
00:34:53.200 --> 00:34:53.554
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

619
00:34:53.554 --> 00:34:59.215
<v AMBROSE>So I know that between in the 1890 he had already passed, because she homestead it.

620
00:34:59.215 --> 00:35:00.985
That much I do know.

621
00:35:00.985 --> 00:35:03.108
I'm sorry what were you asking?

622
00:35:03.108 --> 00:35:07.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Any favorite memories of your grandparents or your great-grandparents even?

623
00:35:07.000 --> 00:35:12.182
<v AMBROSE>My great-grandparents . . . Well for her, she liked the outside.

624
00:35:12.182 --> 00:35:14.485
<v ARSENEAULT>Who is this again?

625
00:35:14.485 --> 00:35:15.636
<v AMBROSE>The Towners.

626
00:35:15.636 --> 00:35:16.788
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay so . . .

627
00:35:16.788 --> 00:35:21.394
<v AMBROSE>[Irie Viola?] Potts Towner and David Duff Towner.

628
00:35:21.394 --> 00:35:23.121
My grandfather's parents.

629
00:35:23.121 --> 00:35:24.848
<v ARSENEAULT>Irie and David?

630
00:35:24.848 --> 00:35:26.000
Okay.

631
00:35:26.000 --> 00:35:27.209
<v AMBROSE>She liked the outside.

632
00:35:27.209 --> 00:35:32.349
She churned and made her own butter and she would . . . Things needed fixing she would fix it.

633
00:35:32.349 --> 00:35:35.372
I think that's where my uncles got their carpentry skills.

634
00:35:35.372 --> 00:35:36.581
<v ARSENEAULT>She would fix it?

635
00:35:36.581 --> 00:35:36.884
<v AMBROSE>Yes.

636
00:35:36.884 --> 00:35:38.093
Grandpa worked and cooked.

637
00:35:38.093 --> 00:35:39.907
He would cook for the family.

638
00:35:39.907 --> 00:35:42.628
Or when we go he would cook for us.

639
00:35:42.628 --> 00:35:44.442
And she would piddle around outside.

640
00:35:44.442 --> 00:35:46.558
She didn't like the inside too much.

641
00:35:46.558 --> 00:35:49.581
<c.p>So that was one of my fond memories of Grandma.

642
00:35:49.581 --> 00:35:51.093
<v ARSENEAULT>What was she doing outside?

643
00:35:51.093 --> 00:35:52.000
She was . . .

644
00:35:52.000 --> 00:35:54.413
<v AMBROSE>She was just doing all kind of things.

645
00:35:54.413 --> 00:35:55.620
Working with her flowers.

646
00:35:55.620 --> 00:35:56.827
Tending to the animals.

647
00:35:56.827 --> 00:35:58.637
Like I said, making her butter.

648
00:35:58.637 --> 00:36:01.352
And I don't know . . . And she read her Bible.

649
00:36:01.352 --> 00:36:02.860
She'd always read her Bible.

650
00:36:02.860 --> 00:36:04.670
That was another thing she did.

651
00:36:04.670 --> 00:36:09.799
Whenever we'd go to visit she would tell us stories from the Bible and read her Bible.

652
00:36:09.799 --> 00:36:13.419
She was big on letting you know what was in the Bible.

653
00:36:13.419 --> 00:36:16.134
But she would just do all kinds of things.

654
00:36:16.134 --> 00:36:22.168
If something . . . If a board was loose or something needed fixing, she would tap it back . . . She would fix it.

655
00:36:22.168 --> 00:36:22.771
You know?

656
00:36:22.771 --> 00:36:24.581
It was just . . . I don't know.

657
00:36:24.581 --> 00:36:26.994
It was just the type person she was.

658
00:36:26.994 --> 00:36:31.821
And I guess she was at home all the time, so when Grandpa was off working,

659
00:36:31.821 --> 00:36:39.363
if there was something that needed to be done, why wait for him to come home and do it when she could do it herself?

660
00:36:39.363 --> 00:36:41.173
And she was taller than him.

661
00:36:41.173 --> 00:36:42.983
<v ARSENEAULT>Like my sister and her husband.

662
00:36:42.983 --> 00:36:43.587
<v AMBROSE>You're kidding!

663
00:36:43.587 --> 00:36:45.397
He was a little short thing.

664
00:36:45.397 --> 00:36:46.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

665
00:36:46.000 --> 00:36:48.158
<v AMBROSE>And Grandma was taller than him.

666
00:36:48.158 --> 00:36:48.877
And was . . .

667
00:36:48.877 --> 00:36:49.956
<v ARSENEAULT>So he cooked.

668
00:36:49.956 --> 00:36:50.675
<v AMBROSE>He cooked.

669
00:36:50.675 --> 00:36:53.193
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of things would he cook?

670
00:36:53.193 --> 00:36:54.272
<v AMBROSE>I don't remember.

671
00:36:54.272 --> 00:37:01.465
I can't remember anything specific because most of the time when we were there we had like lunch or breakfast.

672
00:37:01.465 --> 00:37:06.140
But I don't remember . . . I can't remember anything really specific that he cooked.

673
00:37:06.140 --> 00:37:08.298
But he would do the cooking.

674
00:37:08.298 --> 00:37:15.491
And when we were there for lunch, well Grandma of course would fix us a sandwich or something like that.

675
00:37:15.491 --> 00:37:18.368
Or fix us eggs or something like that.

676
00:37:18.368 --> 00:37:21.965
But she didn't ever cook a big meal for us.

677
00:37:21.965 --> 00:37:24.482
We would eat but it wouldn't be . . .

678
00:37:24.482 --> 00:37:27.000
<v ARSENEAULT>She was too busy fixing things.

679
00:37:27.000 --> 00:37:29.240
<v AMBROSE>Because she was too busy.

680
00:37:29.240 --> 00:37:37.752
She was too busy outside doing stuff she probably needed to be inside doing . . . And she was very clean.

681
00:37:37.752 --> 00:37:39.992
Everything was very, very clean.

682
00:37:39.992 --> 00:37:41.336
Both my great-grandmothers.

683
00:37:41.336 --> 00:37:43.576
You didn't have any clutter.

684
00:37:43.576 --> 00:37:45.816
Everything was just very clean.

685
00:37:45.816 --> 00:37:48.056
I remember that about them.

686
00:37:48.056 --> 00:37:54.776
<v ARSENEAULT>Was that just something in the family or is that just a Mossville, like tradition?

687
00:37:54.776 --> 00:37:57.464
<v AMBROSE>I think it's a family thing.

688
00:37:57.464 --> 00:38:03.736
They just believed cleanliness was next to godliness, and that's the way they believed.

689
00:38:03.736 --> 00:38:06.424
Now they had a small house.

690
00:38:06.424 --> 00:38:09.112
They had a . . . it was two

691
00:38:09.112 --> 00:38:12.248
<c.p>bedrooms, a kitchen, and a living room.

692
00:38:12.248 --> 00:38:20.760
And Grandma Perkins, they had a huge house that had a porch wrapped all the way around the house.

693
00:38:20.760 --> 00:38:23.000
<v ARSENEAULT>They had more money?

694
00:38:23.000 --> 00:38:24.278
<v AMBROSE>I think so.

695
00:38:24.278 --> 00:38:27.686
It was never said, but I think so.

696
00:38:27.686 --> 00:38:28.964
I think so.

697
00:38:28.964 --> 00:38:32.799
I think they had a little bit more money.

698
00:38:32.799 --> 00:38:34.929
I mean they never suffered.

699
00:38:34.929 --> 00:38:38.337
They always had what they needed, you know.

700
00:38:38.337 --> 00:38:43.876
But I think they had . . . The Perkins were a little bit better off.

701
00:38:43.876 --> 00:38:44.728
<v ARSENEAULT>Well what . . .

702
00:38:44.728 --> 00:38:47.710
<v AMBROSE>They were a different kind of people.

703
00:38:47.710 --> 00:38:51.118
The Perkins were more . . . We call them stingy.

704
00:38:51.118 --> 00:38:54.101
But they held on to every dime.

705
00:38:54.101 --> 00:38:57.935
And that side of the family was more giving.

706
00:38:57.935 --> 00:39:00.491
You know, they were more giving.

707
00:39:00.491 --> 00:39:03.047
Love and money and everything else.

708
00:39:03.047 --> 00:39:10.716
So there were two . . . Both loving and kind and sweet, but they were just two different type families.

709
00:39:10.716 --> 00:39:12.846
<v ARSENEAULT>You didn't have a favorite?

710
00:39:12.846 --> 00:39:14.976
<v AMBROSE>Did I have a favorite?

711
00:39:14.976 --> 00:39:15.828
Not really.

712
00:39:15.828 --> 00:39:18.811
They were just both precious to me,

713
00:39:18.811 --> 00:39:27.331
and I didn't really . . . Some of them . . . Some of my cousins had favorites, but I didn't really have a favorite.

714
00:39:27.331 --> 00:39:35.000
I love one . . . These are my greats, so I loved them just as much as the other.

715
00:39:35.000 --> 00:39:40.018
<v ARSENEAULT>I was going to ask you about school and your memories . . .

716
00:39:40.018 --> 00:39:40.930
<v AMBROSE>About school?

717
00:39:40.930 --> 00:39:44.579
We started off in the one room school.

718
00:39:44.579 --> 00:39:47.772
Which was down at the rec center.

719
00:39:47.772 --> 00:39:50.509
Where the rec center is now.

720
00:39:50.509 --> 00:39:53.702
There were three buildings on that property.

721
00:39:53.702 --> 00:39:55.526
They had the cafeteria.

722
00:39:55.526 --> 00:40:00.544
They had this big huge building that was off the ground.

723
00:40:00.544 --> 00:40:01.456
Huge thing.

724
00:40:01.456 --> 00:40:07.386
And there were like two or three elementary grades in that one room.

725
00:40:07.386 --> 00:40:10.123
But it was a big room.

726
00:40:10.123 --> 00:40:27.000
And then there was another little . . . They call them shotgun houses down in New Orleans where they have everything . . . One of those was where the . . . I guess the junior high sixth seventh and eighth graders were.

727
00:40:27.000 --> 00:40:30.055
And we had Ms. Washington was our principal.

728
00:40:30.055 --> 00:40:33.492
And she taught the sixth seventh and eighth graders.

729
00:40:33.492 --> 00:40:36.930
And then we had Ms. [Bilips?] and Ms. Mitchell.

730
00:40:36.930 --> 00:40:41.894
They were the first second and third grade, fourth and fifth grade teachers.

731
00:40:41.894 --> 00:40:47.241
Then of course that went until we moved to the new school in 1955.

732
00:40:47.241 --> 00:40:51.442
Then in 1955 of course we had a bunch of teachers.

733
00:40:51.442 --> 00:40:56.789
But during that time you could only go to the eight grade in Mossville.

734
00:40:56.789 --> 00:41:00.226
Then you had to come over to Lake Charles.

735
00:41:00.226 --> 00:41:07.101
Because my mom went to the . . . She went through her eighth grade, but my mom was real smart.

736
00:41:07.101 --> 00:41:10.920
And Ms. Washington started teaching my mother high school classes.

737
00:41:10.920 --> 00:41:20.849
Like she should have been in the ninth and tenth grade, but she finished her eight grade and she started teaching her the tenth grade curriculum.

738
00:41:20.849 --> 00:41:23.905
And she taught her the eleventh grade curriculum.

739
00:41:23.905 --> 00:41:32.688
So she didn't transfer over here, but she had the . . . what she would have learned in the high schools she had over there.

740
00:41:32.688 --> 00:41:43.000
And I don't how many kids she did that to but I know my mother was one of those that was taught to the eleventh grade.

741
00:41:43.000 --> 00:41:45.400
<v ARSENEAULT>Did your grandparents go to school?

742
00:41:45.400 --> 00:41:46.600
<v AMBROSE>My great . . . Yes.

743
00:41:46.600 --> 00:41:49.400
My great-great-grandparents they were [Irie?] she was

744
00:41:49.400 --> 00:41:55.800
<c.p>educated in the . . . Her mother was a slave and her mother . . . We're trying to find out.

745
00:41:55.800 --> 00:41:57.800
We haven't found out yet.

746
00:41:57.800 --> 00:42:02.600
But her dad was supposedly owned the first restaurant in Lake Charles.

747
00:42:02.600 --> 00:42:07.800
And we haven't found out who he is or what family he is.

748
00:42:07.800 --> 00:42:10.200
We're trying to find that out.

749
00:42:10.200 --> 00:42:12.200
<v ARSENEAULT>He owned the first restaurant?

750
00:42:12.200 --> 00:42:13.000
Or . . .

751
00:42:13.000 --> 00:42:16.049
<v AMBROSE>He owned the first restaurant in Lake Charles.

752
00:42:16.049 --> 00:42:24.435
And he taught . . . They lived in the home with him and when they taught . . . When his kids were taught he taught her.

753
00:42:24.435 --> 00:42:25.578
They taught her.

754
00:42:25.578 --> 00:42:32.058
So she learned to read and write and math, and whatever the other kids learned she learned.

755
00:42:32.058 --> 00:42:34.726
And they were taught in the home.

756
00:42:34.726 --> 00:42:43.874
Grandma Perkins, I don't know where, what school she went to, but, that one up there [points to photograph], she also had an education.

757
00:42:43.874 --> 00:42:48.830
And back during that time, from according to what my grandfather told me,

758
00:42:48.830 --> 00:42:54.547
he my grandfather Author Towner, he told me that he went to the fifth grade.

759
00:42:54.547 --> 00:42:56.453
And I saw a primer.

760
00:42:56.453 --> 00:42:57.978
A third grade primer.

761
00:42:57.978 --> 00:43:05.220
And to this day I'm just so . . . I'm hoping that I'll run across another one that I can purchase.

762
00:43:05.220 --> 00:43:08.269
Either, I guess a flea market or something.

763
00:43:08.269 --> 00:43:10.937
But it was a little blue book.

764
00:43:10.937 --> 00:43:15.892
And that third grade primer had math, in that book they had decimals.

765
00:43:15.892 --> 00:43:17.417
This is third grade.

766
00:43:17.417 --> 00:43:19.323
They had their time tables.

767
00:43:19.323 --> 00:43:20.466
They had fractions.

768
00:43:20.466 --> 00:43:22.372
Because I always asked Daddy.

769
00:43:22.372 --> 00:43:25.040
I said, "Daddy how do you read?

770
00:43:25.040 --> 00:43:29.614
I mean I went through high school, a little college and [Suella?]."

771
00:43:29.614 --> 00:43:33.045
I said, "How do you guys read the ruler?"

772
00:43:33.045 --> 00:43:36.475
I mean he could read it to an inch.

773
00:43:36.475 --> 00:43:38.000
<v ARSENEAULT>He's a carpenter.

774
00:43:38.000 --> 00:43:38.324
<v AMBROSE>Yes.

775
00:43:38.324 --> 00:43:39.297
And just quick.

776
00:43:39.297 --> 00:43:42.865
And I had to sit there and count the little marks.

777
00:43:42.865 --> 00:43:43.189
Of

778
00:43:43.189 --> 00:43:49.027
<c.p>course, you could tell the quarter and the half and the three quarter because it was marked differently.

779
00:43:49.027 --> 00:43:51.622
Well all those other little things you know?

780
00:43:51.622 --> 00:43:52.919
And I told him.

781
00:43:52.919 --> 00:43:55.514
I said, "How do you guys learn that?"

782
00:43:55.514 --> 00:43:58.432
He said, "Well that was part of our math.

783
00:43:58.432 --> 00:44:00.054
We learned to read rulers."

784
00:44:00.054 --> 00:44:05.892
And because that was part of, I guess you could say agricultural or learning to be a carpenter,

785
00:44:05.892 --> 00:44:10.432
because that was what they had to do once they got out of school.

786
00:44:10.432 --> 00:44:12.378
And I said, "You know fractions."

787
00:44:12.378 --> 00:44:14.649
Because he taught us our time tables.

788
00:44:14.649 --> 00:44:16.919
He taught us how to do fractions.

789
00:44:16.919 --> 00:44:19.514
And he only had a fifth grade education.

790
00:44:19.514 --> 00:44:24.054
But when I looked at that book, I realized why, because in third grade,

791
00:44:24.054 --> 00:44:28.919
they had all that stuff we had when we got to fifth and sixth grade.

792
00:44:28.919 --> 00:44:30.216
They'd already had it.

793
00:44:30.216 --> 00:44:38.000
So that they had a whole lot more . . . It took them a shorter time to get, where it took us a longer time.

794
00:44:38.000 --> 00:44:46.235
So that was one of the things I thought was always very interesting about the way they were educated versus the way we were educated.

795
00:44:46.235 --> 00:44:49.859
So you could say, "I had only a third grade education."

796
00:44:49.859 --> 00:44:53.812
But in that third grade education you had like a sixth grade.

797
00:44:53.812 --> 00:44:55.129
And they all read.

798
00:44:55.129 --> 00:45:00.400
And I don't know how many secular books they had, but they all read the Bible.

799
00:45:00.400 --> 00:45:01.718
Everybody read the Bible.

800
00:45:01.718 --> 00:45:05.012
And they read the newspaper, when they got the newspaper.

801
00:45:05.012 --> 00:45:06.329
The newspaper started coming.

802
00:45:06.329 --> 00:45:13.247
Or if they were somewhere where someone had a newspaper, then they would read the newspaper if they didn’t have one.

803
00:45:13.247 --> 00:45:15.224
<v ARSENEAULT>Was there a newspaper in Mossville?

804
00:45:15.224 --> 00:45:17.859
<v AMBROSE>No. We didn't have a newspaper in Mossville.

805
00:45:17.859 --> 00:45:19.506
We had the American Press.

806
00:45:19.506 --> 00:45:23.459
And I don't know how early people started getting the American Press.

807
00:45:23.459 --> 00:45:28.400
I know when I was a teenager, we got the American Press delivered to us.

808
00:45:28.400 --> 00:45:32.353
Well, I guess maybe junior high we started getting the American Press.

809
00:45:32.353 --> 00:45:34.000
But I don't know . . .

810
00:45:34.000 --> 00:45:36.514
<v ARSENEAULT>Was there ever a black newspaper?

811
00:45:36.514 --> 00:45:39.027
<v AMBROSE>And I'm trying to . . . A what?

812
00:45:39.027 --> 00:45:40.284
<v ARSENEAULT>A black newspaper?

813
00:45:40.284 --> 00:45:42.797
<v AMBROSE>No. Not that I know of.

814
00:45:42.797 --> 00:45:44.892
Not that I know of.

815
00:45:44.892 --> 00:45:49.500
And there may have been a paper, a newspaper, in Westlake.

816
00:45:49.500 --> 00:45:50.757
I'm not sure.

817
00:45:50.757 --> 00:45:51.595
Or Sulphur.

818
00:45:51.595 --> 00:45:54.946
I'm not sure, because we didn't get one.

819
00:45:54.946 --> 00:45:59.135
The first one that we got was the American Press.

820
00:45:59.135 --> 00:46:02.905
But I don't remember there being a black newspaper.

821
00:46:02.905 --> 00:46:05.419
We had the radio and TV.

822
00:46:05.419 --> 00:46:12.122
That was our main source of . . . Well, earlier than when we started getting the American Press.

823
00:46:12.122 --> 00:46:20.500
And I don't remember whether they had a . . . And that might be something you could talk to Carol [LaFey?] about,

824
00:46:20.500 --> 00:46:23.014
because she's older than I am.

825
00:46:23.014 --> 00:46:25.946
So she may remember something about that.

826
00:46:25.946 --> 00:46:33.068
And I was trying to think of . . . And I told you something about the churches in Mossville.

827
00:46:33.068 --> 00:46:34.743
We had Mount Zion.

828
00:46:34.743 --> 00:46:40.189
We had a church called The Morning Star, which was a Methodist church.

829
00:46:40.189 --> 00:46:45.635
And then we had Philadelphia, which was a church of God and Christ.

830
00:46:45.635 --> 00:46:48.568
And we had the, Reverend Clofus’s church.

831
00:46:48.568 --> 00:46:51.500
I don't remember what it was called.

832
00:46:51.500 --> 00:46:54.851
I don't think it was called Deliverance then.

833
00:46:54.851 --> 00:46:58.203
And then we had the Pasadena Baptist Church.

834
00:46:58.203 --> 00:46:59.878
Did I missed one?

835
00:46:59.878 --> 00:47:05.324
I think those were the only . . . those were the only churches in Mossville.

836
00:47:05.324 --> 00:47:07.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Was everyone religious?

837
00:47:07.000 --> 00:47:07.378
<v AMBROSE>Yes.

838
00:47:07.378 --> 00:47:07.757
Very.

839
00:47:07.757 --> 00:47:08.514
Very religious.

840
00:47:08.514 --> 00:47:11.541
Well, when you think about back during those

841
00:47:11.541 --> 00:47:14.946
<c.p>times, the church was the hub of the community.

842
00:47:14.946 --> 00:47:16.838
Whatever church you belonged to.

843
00:47:16.838 --> 00:47:19.486
That was the hub of the community.

844
00:47:19.486 --> 00:47:22.892
And the churches . . . You didn't have to be Methodist.

845
00:47:22.892 --> 00:47:24.784
They visited each other's churches.

846
00:47:24.784 --> 00:47:30.838
When something would happen, the Baptist would be in the Methodist church or the Sanctified church.

847
00:47:30.838 --> 00:47:33.865
You would have . . . They kind of meshed together.

848
00:47:33.865 --> 00:47:35.757
So they were very important.

849
00:47:35.757 --> 00:47:36.514
Very important.

850
00:47:36.514 --> 00:47:38.027
Very, very, very important.

851
00:47:38.027 --> 00:47:47.108
Because that's where most of you got together on Sundays, and that's when they heard all the news of what was happening in Mossville,

852
00:47:47.108 --> 00:47:54.297
or through the other churches, and that was where they kind of got all their news for the week.

853
00:47:54.297 --> 00:47:55.811
What was going on.

854
00:47:55.811 --> 00:47:58.081
So that . . . It was very important.

855
00:47:58.081 --> 00:47:58.459
Yes.

856
00:47:58.459 --> 00:48:01.108
<v ARSENEAULT>What if someone didn't go to church?

857
00:48:01.108 --> 00:48:03.000
Did that ever happen?

858
00:48:03.000 --> 00:48:05.646
<v AMBROSE>I guess they just didn't go to church.

859
00:48:05.646 --> 00:48:08.954
I don't think they tarred and feathered them or anything.

860
00:48:08.954 --> 00:48:09.946
I don't know.

861
00:48:09.946 --> 00:48:13.585
My family just always went to church, so I don't know.

862
00:48:13.585 --> 00:48:20.531
And then we had the Catholics, who had to go to church either . . . I think most of those went to Westlake.

863
00:48:20.531 --> 00:48:21.854
To Saint John Bosco.

864
00:48:21.854 --> 00:48:24.831
And then there may have been some in Sulphur.

865
00:48:24.831 --> 00:48:31.446
There was one . . . There was a Catholic Church in Sulphur that I know a lot of the blacks went to.

866
00:48:31.446 --> 00:48:33.762
That was right on the main drag.

867
00:48:33.762 --> 00:48:36.738
I don't know what that street is called now.

868
00:48:36.738 --> 00:48:40.708
But, so a lot of the Catholics went to Sulphur or Westlake.

869
00:48:40.708 --> 00:48:43.354
We didn't have the Catholic Church in Mossville.

870
00:48:43.354 --> 00:48:46.000
<v ARSENEAULT>How many Catholic families lived in Mossville?

871
00:48:46.000 --> 00:48:48.306
<v AMBROSE>Oh gosh I don't know.

872
00:48:48.306 --> 00:48:57.989
I know the LaTours were Catholic, the Guillorys were Catholic, the [Singletons?] were Catholic, the [Carriers?] were Catholic, the Richards were

873
00:48:57.989 --> 00:49:00.294
<c.p>Catholic, the [Gradneys?] were Catholic.

874
00:49:00.294 --> 00:49:05.828
I'm just trying to go down the road, for the different homes.

875
00:49:05.828 --> 00:49:09.056
Those are the ones that I remember.

876
00:49:09.056 --> 00:49:13.206
Those are the ones that I remember were Catholic.

877
00:49:13.206 --> 00:49:19.200
And they probably had some others, but my family, they were all Baptist.

878
00:49:19.200 --> 00:49:21.967
And then we had some Methodists.

879
00:49:21.967 --> 00:49:28.883
But for the most part our family was . . . And we all belonged to Mount Zion.

880
00:49:28.883 --> 00:49:31.189
It was the family church.

881
00:49:31.189 --> 00:49:37.644
And then, did I tell you that we also had a motel in Mossville?

882
00:49:37.644 --> 00:49:40.411
Had a motel on Evergreen Road,

883
00:49:40.411 --> 00:49:51.017
around the corner . . . Well the Remberts had a grocery store and a filling station around the corner of Evergreen and Old Spanish Trail.

884
00:49:51.017 --> 00:49:55.167
And right behind that, they had a little motel.

885
00:49:55.167 --> 00:49:57.011
<v ARSENEAULT>What was it called?

886
00:49:57.011 --> 00:49:57.933
<v AMBROSE>The Remberts.

887
00:49:57.933 --> 00:49:59.778
That was their name.

888
00:49:59.778 --> 00:50:03.928
<v ARSENEAULT>It was the name of the motel as well?

889
00:50:03.928 --> 00:50:09.000
<v AMBROSE>It was the name of the family that owned it.

890
00:50:09.000 --> 00:50:09.833
<v ARSENEUALT>The Rembers?

891
00:50:09.833 --> 00:50:10.667
<v AMBROSE>The Remberts.

892
00:50:10.667 --> 00:50:11.083
R-E-M-B-E-R-T.

893
00:50:11.083 --> 00:50:11.500
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

894
00:50:11.500 --> 00:50:13.167
<v AMBROSE>That was the Remberts.

895
00:50:13.167 --> 00:50:14.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Alright.

896
00:50:14.000 --> 00:50:18.376
<v AMBROSE>And they owned the grocery store, the hotel, and the service station.

897
00:50:18.376 --> 00:50:21.294
That was the first service station in Mossville.

898
00:50:21.294 --> 00:50:22.753
<v ARSENEAULT>What year was that?

899
00:50:22.753 --> 00:50:26.400
<v AMBROSE>Oh God that was before . . . I was a little girl.

900
00:50:26.400 --> 00:50:28.953
So I don't know when it started.

901
00:50:28.953 --> 00:50:29.318
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

902
00:50:29.318 --> 00:50:32.600
<v AMBROSE>But I was a . . . I guess almost a teenager.

903
00:50:32.600 --> 00:50:39.529
And when they passed away, well then the station was sold, or I don't know what happened to it.

904
00:50:39.529 --> 00:50:40.988
I guess torn down.

905
00:50:40.988 --> 00:50:41.718
Because they . . .

906
00:50:41.718 --> 00:50:45.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember the name or the motel?

907
00:50:45.000 --> 00:50:46.913
<v AMBROSE>I don't know that it had a name.

908
00:50:46.913 --> 00:50:47.870
<v ARSENEAULT>Just called it motel?

909
00:50:47.870 --> 00:50:49.304
<v AMBROSE>We just always said, “The Rembert's”.

910
00:50:49.304 --> 00:50:50.022
I don't know.

911
00:50:50.022 --> 00:50:51.935
I don't know if it had a name.

912
00:50:51.935 --> 00:50:52.174
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

913
00:50:52.174 --> 00:50:53.370
<v AMBROSE>I have to ask somebody.

914
00:50:53.370 --> 00:50:54.565
I don't think it did.

915
00:50:54.565 --> 00:50:56.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Who would stay in it?

916
00:50:56.000 --> 00:50:58.219
<v AMBROSE>I guess people coming through Mossville.

917
00:50:58.219 --> 00:51:01.547
I don't know if any plant workers stayed there.

918
00:51:01.547 --> 00:51:04.505
I really don't know who all stayed there.

919
00:51:04.505 --> 00:51:08.203
I'll have to ask my aunt, because she would know.

920
00:51:08.203 --> 00:51:09.312
I don't know.

921
00:51:09.312 --> 00:51:11.900
We just know it was a motel.

922
00:51:11.900 --> 00:51:16.338
It had like, I don't even know how many rooms it had.

923
00:51:16.338 --> 00:51:18.186
Maybe four or five rooms.

924
00:51:18.186 --> 00:51:22.254
And I guess people who were coming in from plant workers,

925
00:51:22.254 --> 00:51:30.019
because during that time we had a lot of people who lived in Opelousas, down East Sunset, Eunice and that area.

926
00:51:30.019 --> 00:51:32.238
They worked here, at the plants.

927
00:51:32.238 --> 00:51:33.717
And they worked construction.

928
00:51:33.717 --> 00:51:35.936
So maybe some of those people.

929
00:51:35.936 --> 00:51:37.045
I don't know.

930
00:51:37.045 --> 00:51:40.373
I was young, I don't even think about it.

931
00:51:40.373 --> 00:51:43.331
I didn't even think about who stayed there.

932
00:51:43.331 --> 00:51:44.810
But we had it.

933
00:51:44.810 --> 00:51:49.617
And then we had another service station that was down by the school.

934
00:51:49.617 --> 00:51:50.727
There were two.

935
00:51:50.727 --> 00:51:52.576
Two service stations down there.

936
00:51:52.576 --> 00:51:59.232
I don't know who owned one of them, but I know one was owned by old [Reece?] Thomas.

937
00:51:59.232 --> 00:52:01.080
He had a body shop.

938
00:52:01.080 --> 00:52:02.190
Car repair shop.

939
00:52:02.190 --> 00:52:04.408
And he had a service station.

940
00:52:04.408 --> 00:52:10.325
Then one right down across from Michigan Avenue on Old Spanish Trail, there was one there.

941
00:52:10.325 --> 00:52:13.283
But I don't know what family owned it.

942
00:52:13.283 --> 00:52:21.418
And then down right off of Prater Road, across from the Mossville Tabernacle, on that corner, there was a service station there.

943
00:52:21.418 --> 00:52:25.116
It was like a Seven Eleven that the Rosses had.

944
00:52:25.116 --> 00:52:27.704
And there was a service station there.

945
00:52:27.704 --> 00:52:32.141
But, I think those were the only service stations that we had.

946
00:52:32.141 --> 00:52:33.621
And they didn't last.

947
00:52:33.621 --> 00:52:35.469
The Rosses' didn't last long.

948
00:52:35.469 --> 00:52:43.974
In fact, he was one of the deacons in our church, and he could no longer be a member of our church because

949
00:52:43.974 --> 00:52:46.563
<c.p>he sold alcohol in his service station.

950
00:52:46.563 --> 00:52:47.302
So that . . .

951
00:52:47.302 --> 00:52:51.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Was that the only place you could get alcohol?

952
00:52:51.000 --> 00:52:52.679
<v AMBROSE>He sold like beer.

953
00:52:52.679 --> 00:52:55.617
I don't think he sold anything else.

954
00:52:55.617 --> 00:52:58.136
We didn't have a liquor store.

955
00:52:58.136 --> 00:53:02.333
They would have to go somewhere else to get liquor.

956
00:53:02.333 --> 00:53:06.531
Or go to the club, because we had The Paradise.

957
00:53:06.531 --> 00:53:10.728
Then, back behind the school, we had The Cowboy Inn.

958
00:53:10.728 --> 00:53:18.704
And then we had Joy Hill, which is on the corner of Trousdale Road and the Old Spanish Trail.

959
00:53:18.704 --> 00:53:21.642
And then we had Low Top Inn.

960
00:53:21.642 --> 00:53:32.136
So they had all these clubs, and behind the Low Top Inn they had a baseball field where they had, what you would consider amateur,

961
00:53:32.136 --> 00:53:40.111
semi-pro, or . . . But it was a big thing, baseball, back during the time when I was a little girl.

962
00:53:40.111 --> 00:53:44.309
That was one of the biggest pastimes that they had.

963
00:53:44.309 --> 00:53:45.568
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it local?

964
00:53:45.568 --> 00:53:46.827
Who played baseball?

965
00:53:46.827 --> 00:53:47.247
<v AMBROSE>Local.

966
00:53:47.247 --> 00:53:48.926
Lake Charles, Westlake, Sulphur.

967
00:53:48.926 --> 00:53:51.025
They would play against DeQuincy.

968
00:53:51.025 --> 00:53:56.481
Whoever had a baseball team in this area they would play against them.

969
00:53:56.481 --> 00:53:57.321
<v ARSENEAULT>What year?

970
00:53:57.321 --> 00:53:59.000
Do you remember?

971
00:53:59.000 --> 00:54:01.324
<v AMBROSE>Gosh I guess in the fifties.

972
00:54:01.324 --> 00:54:02.486
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it integrated?

973
00:54:02.486 --> 00:54:03.261
<v AMBROSE>No. Segregated.

974
00:54:03.261 --> 00:54:03.648
Segregated.

975
00:54:03.648 --> 00:54:05.972
Had to be in the fifties.

976
00:54:05.972 --> 00:54:12.557
I think by the time I graduated from high school, I don't think they were back there.

977
00:54:12.557 --> 00:54:18.368
The old man might have been dead by that time, and the club was closed.

978
00:54:18.368 --> 00:54:24.178
Because I remember by the time I graduated, I don't think Joy Hill was existing.

979
00:54:24.178 --> 00:54:26.889
They had another place called Swamp House.

980
00:54:26.889 --> 00:54:29.213
That was another club they had.

981
00:54:29.213 --> 00:54:31.150
But we had several clubs.

982
00:54:31.150 --> 00:54:36.960
They didn't have to go far to get a drink if they wanted a drink.

983
00:54:36.960 --> 00:54:38.123
It wasn't dry.

984
00:54:38.123 --> 00:54:39.672
Wasn't dry at all.

985
00:54:39.672 --> 00:54:45.095
But I remember the baseball because I remember on Sundays is when I remember,

986
00:54:45.095 --> 00:54:50.518
when I was a little girl, going back there to watch the guys play.

987
00:54:50.518 --> 00:54:56.715
And it would be a lot of people, from all around that would come back there.

988
00:54:56.715 --> 00:55:01.364
Bleachers, and there was a movie on TV that made me remember.

989
00:55:01.364 --> 00:55:03.300
It brought my memories back.

990
00:55:03.300 --> 00:55:09.885
It was . . . I'm trying to think of what the name of it . . . Was it Field of Dreams?

991
00:55:09.885 --> 00:55:13.372
<v ARSENEAULT>Is that about base . . . Is that with Kevin Costner?

992
00:55:13.372 --> 00:55:16.083
<v AMBROSE>Yes it was a long time ago.

993
00:55:16.083 --> 00:55:16.470
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

994
00:55:16.470 --> 00:55:18.407
<v AMBROSE>Where they had the baseball?

995
00:55:18.407 --> 00:55:25.379
But the only thing we just had ours was all black, but it made me think about that.

996
00:55:25.379 --> 00:55:29.640
And I was trying to remember if they played baseball suits.

997
00:55:29.640 --> 00:55:31.577
I guess they must have.

998
00:55:31.577 --> 00:55:36.225
I can't remember, whether they had the actual baseball suits or not.

999
00:55:36.225 --> 00:55:37.000
But . . .

1000
00:55:37.000 --> 00:55:39.660
<v ARSENEAULT>Did the team have a name?

1001
00:55:39.660 --> 00:55:41.876
<v AMBROSE>I don't remember any names.

1002
00:55:41.876 --> 00:55:43.649
Because I was young.

1003
00:55:43.649 --> 00:55:49.412
I guess went to eat and play while the game was going on.

1004
00:55:49.412 --> 00:55:52.072
Because I remember watching the game.

1005
00:55:52.072 --> 00:55:54.732
I just remember we were there.

1006
00:55:54.732 --> 00:55:56.062
<v ARSENEAULT>They sold concessions?

1007
00:55:56.062 --> 00:55:56.505
<v AMBROSE>Yes.

1008
00:55:56.505 --> 00:56:00.052
Like I said it was behind the club.

1009
00:56:00.052 --> 00:56:06.701
Of course we couldn't go in the club but they had food on the grounds.

1010
00:56:06.701 --> 00:56:07.144
Sodas.

1011
00:56:07.144 --> 00:56:12.464
Get sodas and popcorn balls and hotdogs and that kind of thing.

1012
00:56:12.464 --> 00:56:13.351
<v ARSENEAULT>Sounds fun.

1013
00:56:13.351 --> 00:56:14.680
<v AMBROSE>Yes, It was.

1014
00:56:14.680 --> 00:56:15.567
It was.

1015
00:56:15.567 --> 00:56:17.340
<v ARSENEAULT>Did anybody go pro?

1016
00:56:17.340 --> 00:56:20.000
Did any of the players . . .?

1017
00:56:20.000 --> 00:56:22.169
<v AMBROSE>Not that I know of.

1018
00:56:22.169 --> 00:56:26.506
I understand that there was a [Satchel Paige?] from DeQuincy.

1019
00:56:26.506 --> 00:56:30.410
He was one of the, in the Negro league.

1020
00:56:30.410 --> 00:56:36.482
And that was the only one I remember anyone talking about from this area.

1021
00:56:36.482 --> 00:56:42.120
And then there may have been some others, but I don't remember any.

1022
00:56:42.120 --> 00:56:45.590
Only remember him from this area going pro.

1023
00:56:45.590 --> 00:56:52.096
And I had a cousin who, he graduated from Grambling, and he was going pro.

1024
00:56:52.096 --> 00:56:54.265
His name was Richard Lee.

1025
00:56:54.265 --> 00:57:00.771
And the year that he was going to go pro, they had the big strike.

1026
00:57:00.771 --> 00:57:02.506
The football league strike.

1027
00:57:02.506 --> 00:57:06.410
And he came home and he didn't go back.

1028
00:57:06.410 --> 00:57:09.880
He decided he wouldn't be bothered with it.

1029
00:57:09.880 --> 00:57:12.916
But he had been drafted to the

1030
00:57:12.916 --> 00:57:13.349
<c.p>pros.

1031
00:57:13.349 --> 00:57:16.386
But that's the way it goes sometimes.

1032
00:57:16.386 --> 00:57:24.193
<v ARSENEAULT>So with all those clubs in the town and everything was there ever any like crime or trouble?

1033
00:57:24.193 --> 00:57:28.096
<v AMBROSE>You know I was telling you the last time.

1034
00:57:28.096 --> 00:57:31.133
We didn't have a lot of crime.

1035
00:57:31.133 --> 00:57:32.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Right.

1036
00:57:32.000 --> 00:57:35.134
<v AMBROSE>We didn't even have keys for our doors.

1037
00:57:35.134 --> 00:57:35.526
<v ARSENEAULT>Right.

1038
00:57:35.526 --> 00:57:41.010
You said there was no petty crime but the more you learn about it . . .?

1039
00:57:41.010 --> 00:57:46.887
<v AMBROSE>Well back before I was born, I understand that there was some crime in Mossville.

1040
00:57:46.887 --> 00:57:48.454
There was a killing.

1041
00:57:48.454 --> 00:57:49.237
Well, yes.

1042
00:57:49.237 --> 00:57:51.588
I know one person was killed.

1043
00:57:51.588 --> 00:57:53.155
Her husband killed her.

1044
00:57:53.155 --> 00:57:55.505
But that was the only thing.

1045
00:57:55.505 --> 00:57:58.247
That was the only one that I . . .

1046
00:57:58.247 --> 00:58:00.598
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you know who it was?

1047
00:58:00.598 --> 00:58:04.124
<v AMBROSE>Yes it was my . . . It was her sister's daughter.

1048
00:58:04.124 --> 00:58:04.907
Grandma Perkins.

1049
00:58:04.907 --> 00:58:06.082
Her sister's daughter.

1050
00:58:06.082 --> 00:58:10.000
And her husband just kind of went crazy and . . .

1051
00:58:10.000 --> 00:58:11.289
<v ARSENEAULT>And he just . . .?

1052
00:58:11.289 --> 00:58:13.868
<v AMBROSE>And snapped and I don't know.

1053
00:58:13.868 --> 00:58:17.306
We were never . . . Of course we were young.

1054
00:58:17.306 --> 00:58:25.041
We were never told what happened, but he just kind of lost his mind and he killed her.

1055
00:58:25.041 --> 00:58:30.628
And that was really the only one that I can remember right off.

1056
00:58:30.628 --> 00:58:31.917
And then there . . .

1057
00:58:31.917 --> 00:58:33.636
<v ARSENEAULT>That's the only killing?

1058
00:58:33.636 --> 00:58:35.785
<v AMBROSE>There was a young boy.

1059
00:58:35.785 --> 00:58:41.372
Well he was a grown man but I went to school with him.

1060
00:58:41.372 --> 00:58:43.950
He killed his mother and himself.

1061
00:58:43.950 --> 00:58:53.405
And if you had known him, you would never in a million years believe that he could have done something like that.

1062
00:58:53.405 --> 00:58:58.992
In fact, nobody seems to have ever been able to figure out why.

1063
00:58:58.992 --> 00:59:00.281
What made him . . .

1064
00:59:00.281 --> 00:59:03.289
<v ARSENEAULT>Did he leave a note or anything?

1065
00:59:03.289 --> 00:59:08.876
<v AMBROSE>. . . do something . . . No. Smart, a good musician, and he lived in Bel Air.

1066
00:59:08.876 --> 00:59:11.025
His name was Timothy O'Neil.

1067
00:59:11.025 --> 00:59:18.331
That was a shocker for the whole community, because of course I was grown and living here,

1068
00:59:18.331 --> 00:59:24.777
but just to have that happen it was just . . . And he was an only child.

1069
00:59:24.777 --> 00:59:27.355
He was his mother's only child.

1070
00:59:27.355 --> 00:59:35.091
So we don't know if maybe she was sick or he was sick, or you just never know.

1071
00:59:35.091 --> 00:59:41.967
You just never know what goes through somebody's mind for them to do something so radical.

1072
00:59:41.967 --> 00:59:47.124
But I was trying . . . And then, well, I think about it again.

1073
00:59:47.124 --> 00:59:50.562
Before . . . I don't know if I was born.

1074
00:59:50.562 --> 00:59:52.281
I was very young.

1075
00:59:52.281 --> 00:59:54.000
There was another killing.

1076
00:59:54.000 --> 00:59:56.149
It was at a club.

1077
00:59:56.149 --> 00:59:58.298
It was inside the club.

1078
00:59:58.298 --> 01:00:00.017
He was a Moss.

1079
01:00:00.017 --> 01:00:00.446
They

1080
01:00:00.446 --> 01:00:03.455
<c.p>killed . . . The Moss killed a Harry [Mouton?].

1081
01:00:03.455 --> 01:00:07.322
And then, I had a cousin who was killed.

1082
01:00:07.322 --> 01:00:09.041
He was . . . Mistaken identity.

1083
01:00:09.041 --> 01:00:12.479
Someone killed him, taking him for somebody else.

1084
01:00:12.479 --> 01:00:16.347
And I think that was in a club too.

1085
01:00:16.347 --> 01:00:23.653
But now that I'm thinking about it I guess there . . . Well those were one on one crimes.

1086
01:00:23.653 --> 01:00:27.950
But that . . . And those were all when I was young.

1087
01:00:27.950 --> 01:00:31.818
But during the time as a teenager growing up,

1088
01:00:31.818 --> 01:00:40.413
I don't remember anybody . . . I don't remember anybody doing anything like that at the Paradise or in any other clubs.

1089
01:00:40.413 --> 01:00:46.000
Those were all back in the early fifties, sixties, something like that.

1090
01:00:46.000 --> 01:00:47.649
<v ARSENEAULT>What about your great-grandparents?

1091
01:00:47.649 --> 01:00:50.947
Did they ever do any bootlegging or moonshine?

1092
01:00:50.947 --> 01:00:54.246
<v AMBROSE>Oh no. They were too religious for that.

1093
01:00:54.246 --> 01:00:57.956
And if they did, we didn't know about it.

1094
01:00:57.956 --> 01:01:02.079
We never heard anything about it, but I don't know.

1095
01:01:02.079 --> 01:01:04.140
I'm almost sure they didn't.

1096
01:01:04.140 --> 01:01:06.202
I'm almost sure they didn't.

1097
01:01:06.202 --> 01:01:10.325
Probably knew somebody who did, because we never had alcohol.

1098
01:01:10.325 --> 01:01:16.509
At Christmas time my great-grandmother [Lonnie?] she always had a little in her China cabinet.

1099
01:01:16.509 --> 01:01:20.219
She always had a little bottle of Mogen David.

1100
01:01:20.219 --> 01:01:30.526
And after we got to be teenagers she would put us in a little wine glass . . . She'd put us a little drop of Mogen David.

1101
01:01:30.526 --> 01:01:31.763
<v ARSENEAULT>Is that wine?

1102
01:01:31.763 --> 01:01:32.175
<v AMBROSE>Yes.

1103
01:01:32.175 --> 01:01:33.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1104
01:01:33.000 --> 01:01:35.784
<v AMBROSE>And then fill it up with Seven Up.

1105
01:01:35.784 --> 01:01:39.265
So that was as close as we came to alcohol.

1106
01:01:39.265 --> 01:01:42.397
But in our home we didn't have any alcohol.

1107
01:01:42.397 --> 01:01:42.745
Nope.

1108
01:01:42.745 --> 01:01:48.662
Like I said, a little wine and I think that's why Grandma lived . . . In fact I know,

1109
01:01:48.662 --> 01:01:54.578
because my doctor told me that I should drink a glass of wine every evening after dinner.

1110
01:01:54.578 --> 01:01:58.059
And I just haven't found one that I could stomach.

1111
01:01:58.059 --> 01:02:01.539
But my husband's aunt who lived down in New Orleans.

1112
01:02:01.539 --> 01:02:06.760
She would drink after . . . Whenever we would go there, to New Orleans to visit her,

1113
01:02:06.760 --> 01:02:12.676
whenever we got through with lunch or dinner or whatever, she would bring out her little glasses.

1114
01:02:12.676 --> 01:02:17.549
Her little wine glasses and pour us all a little glass of Mogen David.

1115
01:02:17.549 --> 01:02:21.029
And she was a hundred and two when she passed.

1116
01:02:21.029 --> 01:02:26.250
So, I guess maybe is said for a little bit of wine don't hurt anybody.

1117
01:02:26.250 --> 01:02:29.382
And Grandma always had her little bit of wine.

1118
01:02:29.382 --> 01:02:32.167
She'd drink a little bitty glass of wine.

1119
01:02:32.167 --> 01:02:41.216
So I guess she was doing that . . . I don't know if she had high cholesterol or her doctor told her that she need to do it,

1120
01:02:41.216 --> 01:02:44.000
but she always had a little wine.

1121
01:02:44.000 --> 01:02:48.907
<v ARSENEAULT>I know they say it's good for . .. The antioxidants are supposed to be good for you.

1122
01:02:48.907 --> 01:02:49.520
<v AMBROSE>Yes exactly.

1123
01:02:49.520 --> 01:02:51.053
That's what he told me.

1124
01:02:51.053 --> 01:02:55.653
Because I have high cholesterol and he told me if I drink a little wine,

1125
01:02:55.653 --> 01:02:59.947
and then I found out if you drink red or white it don't matter.

1126
01:02:59.947 --> 01:03:00.560
Either one.

1127
01:03:00.560 --> 01:03:02.707
It doesn't matter which one you have.

1128
01:03:02.707 --> 01:03:07.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you grandparents ever tell you any ghost stories or anything like that?

1129
01:03:07.000 --> 01:03:10.356
<v AMBROSE>Oh Lord that was one of our favorite pastimes.

1130
01:03:10.356 --> 01:03:11.475
We loved that.

1131
01:03:11.475 --> 01:03:17.814
I remember in the evening, late in the evening we would all sit out in the yard.

1132
01:03:17.814 --> 01:03:19.678
And that was a big

1133
01:03:19.678 --> 01:03:21.915
<c.p>thing when I was growing up.

1134
01:03:21.915 --> 01:03:23.407
Sitting in the yard.

1135
01:03:23.407 --> 01:03:31.237
We were always in the yard, and I remember before they started getting Avon's . . . What is that stuff Avon was selling?

1136
01:03:31.237 --> 01:03:32.356
Skin So Soft?

1137
01:03:32.356 --> 01:03:37.576
Well, they would make a fire in a tub or a bucket or something,

1138
01:03:37.576 --> 01:03:46.153
and they would put these old wool coats or anything wool that would make a smoke, and that would keep the mosquitoes away.

1139
01:03:46.153 --> 01:03:51.373
So we would sit outside with that, and they would tell us ghost stories.

1140
01:03:51.373 --> 01:04:00.322
And I remember, before I get to the ghost story, Ellison Perkins and his brothers, they all lived in that little area in Mossville.

1141
01:04:00.322 --> 01:04:02.559
Kind of close to each other.

1142
01:04:02.559 --> 01:04:05.169
And they would walk each other home.

1143
01:04:05.169 --> 01:04:09.271
When one would visit the other, they'd walk each other home.

1144
01:04:09.271 --> 01:04:19.712
And they said one night two of them . . . One . . . Grandpa was walking one of the other ones home and when he got about half way there he said,

1145
01:04:19.712 --> 01:04:23.068
“Well”, he said, “Okay I'll walk you back halfway.

1146
01:04:23.068 --> 01:04:25.678
I'll walk you back a little ways”.

1147
01:04:25.678 --> 01:04:29.034
And they walked and talked until it was daylight.

1148
01:04:29.034 --> 01:04:32.390
All night long they were just walking and talking.

1149
01:04:32.390 --> 01:04:35.000
They never did make it home.

1150
01:04:35.000 --> 01:04:43.178
But they would tell us ghost stories about a long time ago and they would tell us that people who had money,

1151
01:04:43.178 --> 01:04:47.267
and they didn't believe in banks, they would hide their money.

1152
01:04:47.267 --> 01:04:52.099
Or they would kill an animal and bury the animal with the money.

1153
01:04:52.099 --> 01:04:57.304
And I guess when they wanted it, then they would go get their money.

1154
01:04:57.304 --> 01:05:01.021
But some of them died with the money still buried.

1155
01:05:01.021 --> 01:05:07.712
And they would have people who would swear . . . In fact, one of my grandfathers, Arthur Towner’s sister's yard,

1156
01:05:07.712 --> 01:05:14.775
right across the street from the Mossville Tabernacle, in the corner of her fence, money was supposedly buried there.

1157
01:05:14.775 --> 01:05:20.351
And they said one night, they heard this noise, like somebody digging with a shovel.

1158
01:05:20.351 --> 01:05:27.042
And they went out there, and one of the ladies from down the street was out there digging.

1159
01:05:27.042 --> 01:05:30.016
Because she was trying to find the money.

1160
01:05:30.016 --> 01:05:39.681
And one of the stories is, if the money wasn't for you, or I guess somebody in your family, well then these ghosts would attack you.

1161
01:05:39.681 --> 01:05:46.000
And we would just sit there and we would be all in awe thinking about that.

1162
01:05:46.000 --> 01:05:48.094
<c.p>You know that stuff wasn't true though.

1163
01:05:48.094 --> 01:05:50.786
But that was what they would tell us anyways.

1164
01:05:50.786 --> 01:05:53.777
That was some of the things they would tell us.

1165
01:05:53.777 --> 01:05:59.760
Or they would tell us, well of course you know about the story of Evergreen Road and the sycamore tree.

1166
01:05:59.760 --> 01:06:04.845
And they had some . . . And I don't know why we weren't afraid to go through the woods,

1167
01:06:04.845 --> 01:06:09.331
because if you had gone, and I wouldn't even try to go through there now,

1168
01:06:09.331 --> 01:06:15.314
but if you’d leave from my house on Old Spanish Trail and go straight through the woods to Highway 90,

1169
01:06:15.314 --> 01:06:19.501
you would go through, there was a piece in there that was pitch black.

1170
01:06:19.501 --> 01:06:24.886
The only thing you could see was the opening at the other end where the daylight came in.

1171
01:06:24.886 --> 01:06:26.979
But it was just black, black, black.

1172
01:06:26.979 --> 01:06:31.167
The mud, the dirt, there was no grass, because grass couldn't grow in there.

1173
01:06:31.167 --> 01:06:35.953
And I don't know how we didn’t . . . How we weren't afraid to even walk through there.

1174
01:06:35.953 --> 01:06:42.235
Or how we weren't afraid that we would walk on a snake or . . . But we never thought about things like that.

1175
01:06:42.235 --> 01:06:43.730
Just never thought about it.

1176
01:06:43.730 --> 01:06:48.516
And I guess if we believed in the ghost I guess we wouldn't have done it.

1177
01:06:48.516 --> 01:06:51.208
Because that would have kept us out of there.

1178
01:06:51.208 --> 01:06:53.900
But it was just dark, dark, dark in there.

1179
01:06:53.900 --> 01:06:56.592
And they had these like palmetto plants in there.

1180
01:06:56.592 --> 01:06:57.490
It looked prehistoric.

1181
01:06:57.490 --> 01:06:59.883
Like you watch these dinosaur things on TV?

1182
01:06:59.883 --> 01:07:01.977
That’s what it looked like in there.

1183
01:07:01.977 --> 01:07:04.370
Yes, but we had lots of ghost stories.

1184
01:07:04.370 --> 01:07:05.566
Lots of ghost stories.

1185
01:07:05.566 --> 01:07:11.249
Back during the time that I grew up they said that people were born with veils on their face.

1186
01:07:11.249 --> 01:07:15.437
It was sometime of skin that was over their face when they were born.

1187
01:07:15.437 --> 01:07:21.120
These were the people who were supposedly the ones who see ghosts and tell you things in the future.

1188
01:07:21.120 --> 01:07:23.214
We never believed in anything like that.

1189
01:07:23.214 --> 01:07:28.000
We just listened to the stories, but we didn't grow up believing anything like that.

1190
01:07:28.000 --> 01:07:30.390
<v ARSENEAULT>Was your family too religious or something?

1191
01:07:30.390 --> 01:07:31.415
<v AMBROSE>I guess so.

1192
01:07:31.415 --> 01:07:32.439
I don't know.

1193
01:07:32.439 --> 01:07:34.829
They just . . . They didn't teach us superstitions.

1194
01:07:34.829 --> 01:07:39.610
The only superstition my grandfather had was when you leave out, don't come back.

1195
01:07:39.610 --> 01:07:41.659
Don't turn around and come back.

1196
01:07:41.659 --> 01:07:45.415
Because he had done that when my grandmother had her stroke.

1197
01:07:45.415 --> 01:07:47.463
<v ARSENEAULT>They didn't have any other ones?

1198
01:07:47.463 --> 01:07:52.244
<v AMBROSE>Of course we had somebody told us not to ever go under a ladder.

1199
01:07:52.244 --> 01:07:58.049
But common sense would tell you if you went under a ladder something might fall on you.

1200
01:07:58.049 --> 01:07:59.756
You know but that was . . .

1201
01:07:59.756 --> 01:08:00.439
<v ARSENEAULT>Common sense.

1202
01:08:00.439 --> 01:08:01.463
<v AMBROSE>Yes, I mean.

1203
01:08:01.463 --> 01:08:03.171
But that was a superstition.

1204
01:08:03.171 --> 01:08:04.878
But my grandfather never did.

1205
01:08:04.878 --> 01:08:11.707
We just didn't . . . We heard about other people having superstitions but . . . Oh the other one we had . . . My grandfather had,

1206
01:08:11.707 --> 01:08:15.122
there wasn't a superstition it was just an old saying.

1207
01:08:15.122 --> 01:08:17.171
Don't ever go to bed mad.

1208
01:08:17.171 --> 01:08:20.585
Whatever you do, make up before you go to bed.

1209
01:08:20.585 --> 01:08:22.293
Which was common sense too.

1210
01:08:22.293 --> 01:08:27.415
Because something could happen to you during the night and you have this guilt feeling.

1211
01:08:27.415 --> 01:08:32.537
That you weren't able to tell somebody that you love them or something like that.

1212
01:08:32.537 --> 01:08:35.610
But those we only . . . Only two things I remember.

1213
01:08:35.610 --> 01:08:38.000
<v ARSENEAULT>They didn't have any other sayings?

1214
01:08:38.000 --> 01:08:43.921
<v AMBROSE>I'm trying to think of some of the other things that I heard as child.

1215
01:08:43.921 --> 01:08:44.316
I

1216
01:08:44.316 --> 01:08:45.895
<c.p>guess we were fortunate.

1217
01:08:45.895 --> 01:08:49.842
We didn't have a lot of crazy stuff around us.

1218
01:08:49.842 --> 01:08:55.368
Because I heard there are a lot of people, that they believed in Voodoo.

1219
01:08:55.368 --> 01:08:56.947
That kind of stuff.

1220
01:08:56.947 --> 01:08:58.526
<v ARSENEAULT>People in Mossville did?

1221
01:08:58.526 --> 01:09:01.684
<v AMBROSE>From what I understand there were some families.

1222
01:09:01.684 --> 01:09:04.053
I don't know who they are.

1223
01:09:04.053 --> 01:09:11.947
Can't tell you who they are, but I've heard that there were some that did believe in stuff like that.

1224
01:09:11.947 --> 01:09:15.895
But that was something that we never, were never taught.

1225
01:09:15.895 --> 01:09:31.289
There was . . . You just hear about it but you know it was something you weren't taught to go and see somebody to tell if . . . The only other thing that . . . And I guess it wasn't really a superstition or anything,

1226
01:09:31.289 --> 01:09:36.026
but I remember people would use home remedies a long time ago.

1227
01:09:36.026 --> 01:09:43.921
They would use . . . They would cross two matches on the top of your head if you had a nose bleed,

1228
01:09:43.921 --> 01:09:46.289
and it would actually stop it.

1229
01:09:46.289 --> 01:09:50.237
They would put cobwebs on cuts to stop the blood.

1230
01:09:50.237 --> 01:09:52.211
It would actually stop it.

1231
01:09:52.211 --> 01:09:59.316
They would . . . There was a root that they would get out of the woods when kids were teething,

1232
01:09:59.316 --> 01:10:02.868
and they would not be worrisome with the teething.

1233
01:10:02.868 --> 01:10:04.053
They did that.

1234
01:10:04.053 --> 01:10:06.421
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of root was it?

1235
01:10:06.421 --> 01:10:08.000
Do you remember?

1236
01:10:08.000 --> 01:10:10.610
<v AMBROSE>I think it was called a sassafras.

1237
01:10:10.610 --> 01:10:12.102
But they did that.

1238
01:10:12.102 --> 01:10:15.831
They would actually go in the woods and get this.

1239
01:10:15.831 --> 01:10:18.814
I don't remember if that stump grows anymore.

1240
01:10:18.814 --> 01:10:21.051
I don't see people with it.

1241
01:10:21.051 --> 01:10:27.390
And I remember when I was a little girl, seeing little babies with dimes on their necks.

1242
01:10:27.390 --> 01:10:29.254
That was for teething too.

1243
01:10:29.254 --> 01:10:32.237
That was supposed to help with the teething.

1244
01:10:32.237 --> 01:10:34.847
So there were a lot of remedies.

1245
01:10:34.847 --> 01:10:38.203
Old time remedies that they were . . . They would use.

1246
01:10:38.203 --> 01:10:39.695
Now my grandmother . . . My

1247
01:10:39.695 --> 01:10:42.678
<c.p>mother's mother, [Verdy?] Towner, she could remove warts.

1248
01:10:42.678 --> 01:10:45.288
Don't ask me how she did it.

1249
01:10:45.288 --> 01:10:48.644
I only found that out since I've been grown.

1250
01:10:48.644 --> 01:10:54.610
I have one of my great-aunts tell me that she had a wart on her foot.

1251
01:10:54.610 --> 01:10:56.475
Or mole on her foot.

1252
01:10:56.475 --> 01:10:59.831
And my grandmother prayed for her, and it stopped.

1253
01:10:59.831 --> 01:11:02.441
And there's another thing in our family.

1254
01:11:02.441 --> 01:11:05.424
There . . . My great-grandmother, [Irie?], she could stop bleeding.

1255
01:11:05.424 --> 01:11:08.407
Now how she did it, I don't know.

1256
01:11:08.407 --> 01:11:14.000
But she had to . . . When . . . Before Grandma died, she passed it down to my cousin.

1257
01:11:14.000 --> 01:11:14.746
Her grandson.

1258
01:11:14.746 --> 01:11:19.593
So I don't know if it's . . . And he has to pass it down.

1259
01:11:19.593 --> 01:11:21.085
He can stop bleeding.

1260
01:11:21.085 --> 01:11:25.932
And I didn't know that, until one of my cousins told me that.

1261
01:11:25.932 --> 01:11:28.915
We were talking about things in the family.

1262
01:11:28.915 --> 01:11:32.271
He said, "Well you know Grammy could stop bleeding."

1263
01:11:32.271 --> 01:11:33.763
I said, "Do what?"

1264
01:11:33.763 --> 01:11:36.000
<v ARSENEAULT>In herself or for others?

1265
01:11:36.000 --> 01:11:37.016
<v AMBROSE>For other people.

1266
01:11:37.016 --> 01:11:38.032
For other people.

1267
01:11:38.032 --> 01:11:40.403
And I said, "What did she do?"

1268
01:11:40.403 --> 01:11:43.113
He said, "I don't know what she did."

1269
01:11:43.113 --> 01:11:52.597
He said, "But before she died she passed it on to Wilford and told Wilford he had to find somebody in the family to pass it on to."

1270
01:11:52.597 --> 01:11:55.645
I don't know if this is just through prayer.

1271
01:11:55.645 --> 01:11:57.339
People that pray for you.

1272
01:11:57.339 --> 01:11:58.355
Pray certain prayers.

1273
01:11:58.355 --> 01:12:00.387
I don't know how it worked.

1274
01:12:00.387 --> 01:12:03.097
So Wilford has that, he can do it.

1275
01:12:03.097 --> 01:12:07.500
So now before he passes on, he has to pass it to somebody.

1276
01:12:07.500 --> 01:12:15.968
And I don't know if it's a woman has to pass it to a guy and a guy has to pass it to another female.

1277
01:12:15.968 --> 01:12:19.694
I don't know how, if it words like that or what.

1278
01:12:19.694 --> 01:12:23.081
But that was one of the things in our family.

1279
01:12:23.081 --> 01:12:27.484
And then John [Israel?] Towner, his father [ Duff’s?] father, he was a seer.

1280
01:12:27.484 --> 01:12:29.177
And he used tea leaves.

1281
01:12:29.177 --> 01:12:33.919
And people would lose things and they would come to him to find them.

1282
01:12:33.919 --> 01:12:35.952
How that happened I don't know.

1283
01:12:35.952 --> 01:12:37.984
<v ARSENEAULT>How did he get that talent?

1284
01:12:37.984 --> 01:12:39.000
That skill?

1285
01:12:39.000 --> 01:12:40.230
<v AMBROSE>I don't know.

1286
01:12:40.230 --> 01:12:42.691
It was just born with it.

1287
01:12:42.691 --> 01:12:45.972
I don't think he was ever taught it.

1288
01:12:45.972 --> 01:12:50.073
Because his mom and dad died when he was young.

1289
01:12:50.073 --> 01:12:54.994
And how he found out that he could do stuff like that?

1290
01:12:54.994 --> 01:12:59.916
I don't know that either, because he died in thirty-seven I think.

1291
01:12:59.916 --> 01:13:02.787
So I don't . . . I never knew him.

1292
01:13:02.787 --> 01:13:06.478
And my mom never said how he got that.

1293
01:13:06.478 --> 01:13:10.579
He's the one that's on the front of the page.

1294
01:13:10.579 --> 01:13:11.809
Toward the front.

1295
01:13:11.809 --> 01:13:12.629
His picture.

1296
01:13:12.629 --> 01:13:14.270
<v ARSENEAULT>He was a seer?

1297
01:13:14.270 --> 01:13:14.680
<v AMBROSE>Yes.

1298
01:13:14.680 --> 01:13:17.961
He was half Cherokee Indian and half black.

1299
01:13:17.961 --> 01:13:27.393
But how he found that out . . . And I guess I need to ask my grandfather has one cousin, first cousin, that's still living.

1300
01:13:27.393 --> 01:13:34.775
And I don't know if she'd remember or not, but she was reared in his house with him.

1301
01:13:34.775 --> 01:13:40.107
And I need to ask her how . . . If he ever talked about that.

1302
01:13:40.107 --> 01:13:45.848
Most times when older people had stuff like that, they didn’t talk about it.

1303
01:13:45.848 --> 01:13:48.719
It wasn't something that you talked about.

1304
01:13:48.719 --> 01:13:49.949
I don't know.

1305
01:13:49.949 --> 01:13:52.000
<v ARSENEAULT>He read tea leaves?

1306
01:13:52.000 --> 01:13:52.615
<v AMBROSE>Tea leaves.

1307
01:13:52.615 --> 01:13:57.846
He said whenever . . . My aunt . . . My cousin said that whenever somebody would come and want that done,

1308
01:13:57.846 --> 01:14:01.231
and she said when she was little, she didn't really know,

1309
01:14:01.231 --> 01:14:06.154
she said as she got to be a grown woman, she realized what he was doing.

1310
01:14:06.154 --> 01:14:09.846
But some . . . Somebody would come for him to . . . When they lost something.

1311
01:14:09.846 --> 01:14:13.538
He would tell her to go put the pot of tea on.

1312
01:14:13.538 --> 01:14:16.308
And she said she just went and did it.

1313
01:14:16.308 --> 01:14:18.769
But now she . . . And then he said . . . She

1314
01:14:18.769 --> 01:14:23.385
<c.p>said, she would serve them their tea and then she'd go on out to play.

1315
01:14:23.385 --> 01:14:27.077
Because you didn't stay around grown folks when grown folks were talking.

1316
01:14:27.077 --> 01:14:29.231
And she'd go on out to play.

1317
01:14:29.231 --> 01:14:32.000
But she said now she realizes what it was.

1318
01:14:32.000 --> 01:14:33.846
<v ARSENEAULT>He was looking at the leaves?

1319
01:14:33.846 --> 01:14:35.692
And the leaves would tell him?

1320
01:14:35.692 --> 01:14:37.846
<v AMBROSE>I guess when they . . . He drinks them.

1321
01:14:37.846 --> 01:14:45.846
I don't know if he had to drink the tea and saw the leaves or if he could just look at the leaves through the tea.

1322
01:14:45.846 --> 01:14:52.615
I don't know, because he was dead long before I was born so I don't know how he . . . how he did it.

1323
01:14:52.615 --> 01:14:56.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you have any other like healers in your family?

1324
01:14:56.000 --> 01:15:00.000
<v AMBROSE>I was trying to think if we had any others.

1325
01:15:00.000 --> 01:15:08.400
And I don't . . . You know they weren't . . . They weren't the kind where you had people coming to them all the time,

1326
01:15:08.400 --> 01:15:11.600
or they would charge for it or anything.

1327
01:15:11.600 --> 01:15:22.000
It's just if they knew that you had the problem, or if you call them, like somebody if somebody like grandma with her kids or grandkids,

1328
01:15:22.000 --> 01:15:27.600
if somebody called her and tell her that somebody cut themselves and was bleeding,

1329
01:15:27.600 --> 01:15:35.600
couldn't stop the bleeding or hurt themselves and couldn’t stop the bleeding, well then she would take care of it.

1330
01:15:35.600 --> 01:15:38.000
<v ARSENEAULT>She would just pray for them?

1331
01:15:38.000 --> 01:15:38.400
<v AMBROSE>Yes.

1332
01:15:38.400 --> 01:15:43.200
But it wasn't like you know how some people charge money and . . .

1333
01:15:43.200 --> 01:15:43.600
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

1334
01:15:43.600 --> 01:15:46.400
Were there any of those in Mossville?

1335
01:15:46.400 --> 01:15:48.000
<v AMBROSE>Not that I remember.

1336
01:15:48.000 --> 01:15:52.000
And if there were we were too young to know.

1337
01:15:52.000 --> 01:15:55.600
Nobody . . . You know, we didn't know anything about it.

1338
01:15:55.600 --> 01:15:58.400
But I don't remember anybody in Mossville.

1339
01:15:58.400 --> 01:16:03.200
And like I said I don't remember anybody who fooled with Voodoo.

1340
01:16:03.200 --> 01:16:08.800
They had some here in Lake Charles, but I don't remember anybody in Mossville.

1341
01:16:08.800 --> 01:16:16.400
And the only way you knew them in Lake Charles because they would have like things on their house.

1342
01:16:16.400 --> 01:16:20.000
You know like maybe a . . . I don't know.

1343
01:16:20.000 --> 01:16:21.282
<v ARSENEAULT>A symbol or something?

1344
01:16:21.282 --> 01:16:26.728
<v AMBROSE>Yes things . . . Or things written on a board or something where you could . . . Those that charge money.

1345
01:16:26.728 --> 01:16:27.369
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh okay.

1346
01:16:27.369 --> 01:16:30.252
<v AMBROSE>And then I think . . . But I never knew any.

1347
01:16:30.252 --> 01:16:31.854
I didn't know them personally.

1348
01:16:31.854 --> 01:16:34.097
We just passing you could see it.

1349
01:16:34.097 --> 01:16:36.660
But I don't know of anybody in Mossville.

1350
01:16:36.660 --> 01:16:42.427
I told my sister she should have come and been with me because she might have remembered something.

1351
01:16:42.427 --> 01:16:46.272
But stuff like that I don't think she would have remembered either.

1352
01:16:46.272 --> 01:16:51.078
Because we never talked about . . . Stuff like that wasn't ever talked about in the family.

1353
01:16:51.078 --> 01:16:53.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Is she your older sister?

1354
01:16:53.000 --> 01:16:54.622
<v AMBROSE>No she's younger than me.

1355
01:16:54.622 --> 01:16:55.919
But she was bad.

1356
01:16:55.919 --> 01:16:59.486
So she got around a little bit more than I did.

1357
01:16:59.486 --> 01:17:00.784
So she was mischievous.

1358
01:17:00.784 --> 01:17:02.405
Let’s say it like that.

1359
01:17:02.405 --> 01:17:08.568
But no, I don't know of anybody in Mossville that . . . And I had a cousin tell me one time.

1360
01:17:08.568 --> 01:17:12.784
In fact, this has been in recent years before the plant shut down.

1361
01:17:12.784 --> 01:17:17.000
That there was somebody in Mossville but she was . . . I said, "Well who?"

1362
01:17:17.000 --> 01:17:18.946
He said, "I can't tell you."

1363
01:17:18.946 --> 01:17:20.892
She said, "Because they might know."

1364
01:17:20.892 --> 01:17:25.432
I said, "How are they going to know you telling me who they are?"

1365
01:17:25.432 --> 01:17:27.054
She said, "I don't know."

1366
01:17:27.054 --> 01:17:31.270
So then I found out that she believed in that kind of stuff.

1367
01:17:31.270 --> 01:17:33.216
Because now why not tell me?

1368
01:17:33.216 --> 01:17:36.135
<v ARSENEAULT>A spirit is going to tell them or something?

1369
01:17:36.135 --> 01:17:37.108
<v AMBROSE>I don't know.

1370
01:17:37.108 --> 01:17:39.054
I hadn't figured that out yet.

1371
01:17:39.054 --> 01:17:41.973
I said, "You and I are sitting here talking."

1372
01:17:41.973 --> 01:17:42.297
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

1373
01:17:42.297 --> 01:17:47.811
<v AMBROSE>If I'm not afraid to hear who it is why should you be afraid to tell me?

1374
01:17:47.811 --> 01:17:51.703
I just . . . But she never would tell me so I don't know.

1375
01:17:51.703 --> 01:17:53.000
Till this day.

1376
01:17:53.000 --> 01:17:55.286
<v ARSENEAULT>What was that person supposed to be doing?

1377
01:17:55.286 --> 01:18:00.714
<v AMBROSE>I don't know I guess he told fortunes and . . . I don't really know because she never would tell me.

1378
01:18:00.714 --> 01:18:02.143
She just told me that.

1379
01:18:02.143 --> 01:18:09.286
I said, "Well now you going to tell me that much and then don't tell me the rest and you know I'm wanting to know."

1380
01:18:09.286 --> 01:18:10.429
But I don't know.

1381
01:18:10.429 --> 01:18:11.286
She never did

1382
01:18:11.286 --> 01:18:11.857
<c.p>tell me.

1383
01:18:11.857 --> 01:18:15.857
So I guess somebody else will have to tell me one of these days.

1384
01:18:15.857 --> 01:18:20.143
<v ARSENEAULT>I think we already talked about like medical treatment and stuff when y'all were younger?

1385
01:18:20.143 --> 01:18:22.143
Like where you went for medical treatment?

1386
01:18:22.143 --> 01:18:23.000
<v AMBROSE>Yes Doctor Fellows.

1387
01:18:23.000 --> 01:18:24.429
<v ARSENEAULT>We talked about that already?

1388
01:18:24.429 --> 01:18:25.000
<v AMBROSE>Yes.

1389
01:18:25.000 --> 01:18:25.385
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

1390
01:18:25.385 --> 01:18:27.698
<v AMBROSE>And we had the Sulphur hospital.

1391
01:18:27.698 --> 01:18:28.854
And Doctor Fellows.

1392
01:18:28.854 --> 01:18:31.938
I told you he would make house calls?

1393
01:18:31.938 --> 01:18:32.323
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

1394
01:18:32.323 --> 01:18:41.188
<v AMBROSE>And of course we had some of the people that lived here would go to a Doctor Ross who was a black doctor,

1395
01:18:41.188 --> 01:18:42.729
here in Lake Charles.

1396
01:18:42.729 --> 01:18:44.656
Doctor Ross and Doctor Martin.

1397
01:18:44.656 --> 01:18:49.667
There were two black doctors here that some of the people would see.

1398
01:18:49.667 --> 01:18:51.979
<v ARSENEAULT>I think we got that already.

1399
01:18:51.979 --> 01:18:53.521
We talked about church.

1400
01:18:53.521 --> 01:18:55.448
Did I ask you about

1401
01:18:55.448 --> 01:18:57.760
<c.p>who made decisions in your family?

1402
01:18:57.760 --> 01:18:59.688
Did we talk about that?

1403
01:18:59.688 --> 01:19:02.000
<v AMBROSE>My mother and my grandfather.

1404
01:19:02.000 --> 01:19:02.371
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1405
01:19:02.371 --> 01:19:06.457
<v AMBROSE>We didn't talk about that, but my mother and my grandfather.

1406
01:19:06.457 --> 01:19:10.543
And I think in most families, the mothers were the disciplinarians.

1407
01:19:10.543 --> 01:19:11.657
In my family.

1408
01:19:11.657 --> 01:19:14.629
The moms always took care of the whipping.

1409
01:19:14.629 --> 01:19:17.229
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes was it just whippings usually or . . . ?

1410
01:19:17.229 --> 01:19:20.200
<v AMBROSE>And we didn't get put in the corner.

1411
01:19:20.200 --> 01:19:21.314
We got spankings.

1412
01:19:21.314 --> 01:19:23.914
And I don't think it hurt anybody.

1413
01:19:23.914 --> 01:19:26.143
And my sister told my mom.

1414
01:19:26.143 --> 01:19:31.343
She said, "Well I guess I need to go and call them on you."

1415
01:19:31.343 --> 01:19:33.200
Now she's grown and married.

1416
01:19:33.200 --> 01:19:37.286
But she said, "Because you were . . ." What did she tell her?

1417
01:19:37.286 --> 01:19:38.771
"Because you abused me."

1418
01:19:38.771 --> 01:19:41.000
<v ARSENEAULT>She's telling your mom that?

1419
01:19:41.000 --> 01:19:47.538
<v AMBROSE>Yes, but I think she caught more whippings than any of the rest of us.

1420
01:19:47.538 --> 01:19:54.949
Because she was always doing something she didn't have any business . . . But they didn't spare the rod.

1421
01:19:54.949 --> 01:19:58.000
And it didn't hurt any of us.

1422
01:19:58.000 --> 01:20:02.795
In fact, I think we were all the better for it.

1423
01:20:02.795 --> 01:20:08.462
<v ARSENEAULT>And like who made the decision about like finances and who got like . . ?

1424
01:20:08.462 --> 01:20:10.641
<v AMBROSE>My mother and my grandfather.

1425
01:20:10.641 --> 01:20:11.077
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1426
01:20:11.077 --> 01:20:11.513
<v AMBROSE>Yes.

1427
01:20:11.513 --> 01:20:15.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Y'all lived in the same house together?

1428
01:20:15.000 --> 01:20:16.494
<v AMBROSE>In the same house.

1429
01:20:16.494 --> 01:20:17.614
With my grandfather.

1430
01:20:17.614 --> 01:20:19.854
And my mother took . . . In fact,

1431
01:20:19.854 --> 01:20:35.165
I guess my mother made most of the decisions about the household because my grandfather just kind of handed everything over to her to . . . Because my . . . When my grandmother died [Berty May?] we . . . She had . . . My mother had a little sister,

1432
01:20:35.165 --> 01:20:37.032
who was six years old.

1433
01:20:37.032 --> 01:20:42.633
So when my grandmother died, well then my mother just . . . She was reared with us.

1434
01:20:42.633 --> 01:20:45.620
She just took over the rearing of her.

1435
01:20:45.620 --> 01:20:50.848
And my grandfather was there, but my mother was her mama too, you know.

1436
01:20:50.848 --> 01:20:55.703
So my grandfather, he never . . . We knew what the rules and regulations were,

1437
01:20:55.703 --> 01:21:01.304
that he had, and we knew what he felt about it so we just followed.

1438
01:21:01.304 --> 01:21:03.171
We knew what to do.

1439
01:21:03.171 --> 01:21:09.892
But then if we ever got out of hand, well then my mother would take care of us.

1440
01:21:09.892 --> 01:21:14.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of like values did they instill in you?

1441
01:21:14.000 --> 01:21:15.244
<v AMBROSE>Oh my God.

1442
01:21:15.244 --> 01:21:15.659
Honesty.

1443
01:21:15.659 --> 01:21:19.805
They believed if you lie you'll steal if you'll steal

1444
01:21:19.805 --> 01:21:20.634
<c.p>you'll kill.

1445
01:21:20.634 --> 01:21:22.293
<v ARSENEAULT>If you what now?

1446
01:21:22.293 --> 01:21:24.366
<v AMBROSE>If you lie you'll steal.

1447
01:21:24.366 --> 01:21:26.439
If you steal you'll kill.

1448
01:21:26.439 --> 01:21:28.927
They believed in the Ten Commandments.

1449
01:21:28.927 --> 01:21:31.829
They believed that . . . They believed in humility.

1450
01:21:31.829 --> 01:21:38.049
That you should be humble and not think that you were more than anybody else.

1451
01:21:38.049 --> 01:21:43.439
You might have been smarter, but there was always somebody smarter than you.

1452
01:21:43.439 --> 01:21:48.829
And my mother was a big stickler on “pretty is as pretty does.”

1453
01:21:48.829 --> 01:21:53.805
You could be pretty, pretty but you could be so ugly inside.

1454
01:21:53.805 --> 01:21:57.537
And she never wanted us to be that way.

1455
01:21:57.537 --> 01:21:59.610
And my mother taught us.

1456
01:21:59.610 --> 01:22:01.268
She taught us morals.

1457
01:22:01.268 --> 01:22:05.000
She taught us how to treat other people.

1458
01:22:05.000 --> 01:22:08.901
She taught us how we were supposed to be ladies.

1459
01:22:08.901 --> 01:22:12.803
That we were never to go around with no slip.

1460
01:22:12.803 --> 01:22:14.753
That was a big thing.

1461
01:22:14.753 --> 01:22:19.825
And when my mom died, my little cousin got up and said that.

1462
01:22:19.825 --> 01:22:21.386
She said, "Oh gosh."

1463
01:22:21.386 --> 01:22:31.919
She said, "My [ . . .?] if she knew that I didn't have a slip on because I don't . . ." She was getting ready to go show something for her school.

1464
01:22:31.919 --> 01:22:37.771
And she got all dressed and was all cute and didn't have a slip on.

1465
01:22:37.771 --> 01:22:40.112
My mother just [read her beads?].

1466
01:22:40.112 --> 01:22:44.013
So she taught us how we were supposed to dress.

1467
01:22:44.013 --> 01:22:46.744
How we were supposed to be dignified.

1468
01:22:46.744 --> 01:22:47.915
Prim and proper.

1469
01:22:47.915 --> 01:22:50.256
So that's what she taught us.

1470
01:22:50.256 --> 01:22:52.987
And we had everything that we needed.

1471
01:22:52.987 --> 01:22:58.448
We didn't have an abundance of anything, but we had everything that we needed.

1472
01:22:58.448 --> 01:23:02.740
And whenever we needed something, she saw that we got it.

1473
01:23:02.740 --> 01:23:05.081
That was for church or school.

1474
01:23:05.081 --> 01:23:07.031
We participated in all activities.

1475
01:23:07.031 --> 01:23:12.883
There was nothing that we weren't allowed or she told us no we couldn’t do.

1476
01:23:12.883 --> 01:23:17.175
I was Ms. Mossville, and my mom got what I needed.

1477
01:23:17.175 --> 01:23:23.417
And I remember that I was always heavy busted and she had to go to [Muller’s?].

1478
01:23:23.417 --> 01:23:25.758
There was a department store here.

1479
01:23:25.758 --> 01:23:28.099
My evening gown had to be

1480
01:23:28.099 --> 01:23:28.489
<c.p>altered.

1481
01:23:28.489 --> 01:23:29.269
Yes that's . . .

1482
01:23:29.269 --> 01:23:32.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did she make it for you?

1483
01:23:32.000 --> 01:23:33.458
<v AMBROSE>No, she bought it there.

1484
01:23:33.458 --> 01:23:36.667
It was a wedding dress, and she had them alter it.

1485
01:23:36.667 --> 01:23:38.708
Had the sleeves cut out of it.

1486
01:23:38.708 --> 01:23:41.333
I don't have . . . I had a picture of it.

1487
01:23:41.333 --> 01:23:43.375
<v ARSENEAULT>I would love to see that picture.

1488
01:23:43.375 --> 01:23:45.125
<v AMBROSE>I don't know where it is.

1489
01:23:45.125 --> 01:23:47.750
I thought it was in that book there but . . .

1490
01:23:47.750 --> 01:23:48.333
<v ARSENEAULT>It's not?

1491
01:23:48.333 --> 01:23:49.792
Is it in here or . . .?

1492
01:23:49.792 --> 01:23:50.083
No?

1493
01:23:50.083 --> 01:23:56.208
<v AMBROSE>And when I went to California I had a cousin who lived in Long Beach and we went to his restaurant.

1494
01:23:56.208 --> 01:23:57.958
And he called his restaurant Seven-Oh-Six-Six-Three.

1495
01:23:57.958 --> 01:24:00.000
<v ARSENEAULT>I remember you telling me that.

1496
01:24:00.000 --> 01:24:02.388
<v AMBROSE>And my picture was in there on the wall.

1497
01:24:02.388 --> 01:24:04.245
And he had all this Mossville memorabilia.

1498
01:24:04.245 --> 01:24:05.306
All over the place.

1499
01:24:05.306 --> 01:24:08.490
And I said, "Oh my God I want to steal it back."

1500
01:24:08.490 --> 01:24:08.755
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

1501
01:24:08.755 --> 01:24:10.612
<v AMBROSE>My sister had given it to him.

1502
01:24:10.612 --> 01:24:13.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh maybe she can send you . . . Maybe you can . . .

1503
01:24:13.000 --> 01:24:16.184
<v AMBROSE>I think I have one here I'll have to look for it.

1504
01:24:16.184 --> 01:24:16.449
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

1505
01:24:16.449 --> 01:24:17.510
Love to see that.

1506
01:24:17.510 --> 01:24:18.837
Do you know what time?

1507
01:24:18.837 --> 01:24:19.102
Okay.

1508
01:24:19.102 --> 01:24:23.878
Do you want to talk a little bit more and then we can kind of wrap it up?

1509
01:24:23.878 --> 01:24:26.000
I know it's getting kind of late.

1510
01:24:26.000 --> 01:24:27.765
<v AMBROSE>If you have some other questions?

1511
01:24:27.765 --> 01:24:28.353
<v ARSENEAULT>I do.

1512
01:24:28.353 --> 01:24:30.412
I might have to do another one.

1513
01:24:30.412 --> 01:24:31.294
Is that okay?

1514
01:24:31.294 --> 01:24:31.588
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1515
01:24:31.588 --> 01:24:33.059
<v AMBROSE>We can do another one.

1516
01:24:33.059 --> 01:24:33.647
I'm available.

1517
01:24:33.647 --> 01:24:33.941
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1518
01:24:33.941 --> 01:24:35.118
<v AMBROSE>I'll make myself available.

1519
01:24:35.118 --> 01:24:36.882
<v ARSENEAULT>Then I think that's a good . . .

1520
01:24:36.882 --> 01:24:41.000
<v AMBROSE>And I'm going to try to have everything done for the next one.

1521
01:24:41.000 --> 01:24:42.164
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh it's not a rush.

1522
01:24:42.164 --> 01:24:44.959
<v AMBROSE>I'm going to try and have you . . . Have your picture copies made.

1523
01:24:44.959 --> 01:24:45.192
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1524
01:24:45.192 --> 01:24:45.658
<v AMBROSE>Of everybody.

1525
01:24:45.658 --> 01:24:47.288
And I'm going to have them labeled.

1526
01:24:47.288 --> 01:24:47.521
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1527
01:24:47.521 --> 01:24:48.918
I mean that would be great.

1528
01:24:48.918 --> 01:24:51.945
But I mean I don't want you to have to go through any . . .

1529
01:24:51.945 --> 01:24:53.342
<v AMBROSE>So you'll know who they are.

1530
01:24:53.342 --> 01:24:53.575
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1531
01:24:53.575 --> 01:24:55.904
Well thank you so much for talking with us again.

1532
01:24:55.904 --> 01:24:56.603
<v AMBROSE>Well you're welcome.

1533
01:24:56.603 --> 01:24:57.301
<v ARSENEAULT>It's so great.

1534
01:24:57.301 --> 01:24:58.000
<v AMBROSE>You're welcome.

1535
01:24:58.000 --> 01:24:58.200
<c.p>[End Tape 4485.

1536
01:24:58.000 --> 01:24:58.200
End Session III.]
